WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.000
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Right, well, welcome, everybody.
Apologies for absence: we’ve got Wendy,

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:15.299
I’m afraid, has got a problem at home; Joanne
has got flu; and Michelle is away, so we’ve

00:00:15.299 --> 00:00:19.360
got three apologies for absence today.
Any declarations of interest that we – that

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:20.360
anyone needs to raise? Yeah?
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: Could I declare an interest

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:27.640
on the – item eight on the agenda? I work
with a company, Incisive Health, that also

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:34.980
advises pharmaceutical companies.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay. Thanks, Richard.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:42.320
Are people happy with the minutes? I wasn’t
here, so I’m neither happy nor unhappy with

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.560
them. Okay. So, we can approve the minutes,
please.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:53.140
So, my report. I think I’d just like to
say two things at the beginning. One is that

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:58.860
we are going to be saying goodbye to Jane,
Moira and Victor, who will be standing down,

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:04.549
and I think there’s an occasion this evening
when we can say – or some people can say

00:01:04.549 --> 00:01:08.990
more about all three of you. But maybe just
on Jane’s – Jane, I think you’ve been

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:11.840
in the NHS for 40 years.
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: Nearly, yes.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:17.780
&gt;&gt;David Prior: So, that is a remarkable contribution
to the NHS, so thank you very much for that;

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:21.610
that’s fantastic. And to Moira and Victor:
I’m going to say a few more words about

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:26.300
Victor at the end, because Victor’s not
coming to the dinner this evening, but I know

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:34.010
Moira, you’re going to the dinner this evening.
So, thank you to all three of you for the

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:36.050
contributions you’ve made.
And just – for me, this is my first meeting,

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:42.970
and just to say that that is a – it’s
a huge privilege to be Chairman of NHS England.

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:47.659
It’s – the NHS is such an important institution
for the whole country, and to be a part of

00:01:47.659 --> 00:01:53.510
it is a fantastic – well, a privilege and
opportunity for me. So, I’m really pleased

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:57.060
to be here.
So, that’s what I’m going to say by way

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:02.891
of my report. Simon, how about you?
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Thanks, David. So, three

00:02:02.891 --> 00:02:09.270
things, really. Since we last had a public
Board meeting, we’ve had the Budget. And

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:16.019
in that, the Chancellor, as you know, confirmed
the £20.5&nbsp;billion revenue increase for NHS

00:02:16.019 --> 00:02:23.409
England by ‘23/24 that was set out back
in June. As part of that, he also confirmed

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:29.840
that we would continue with the mental health
investment guarantee for the next five years,

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:36.909
whereby mental-health spending will go up
as a share of NHS funding over that period.

00:02:36.909 --> 00:02:43.609
And last week, I accompanied the Prime Minister
to visit two community health services in

00:02:43.609 --> 00:02:50.329
North London, where she set out a similar
commitment for primary medical and community

00:02:50.329 --> 00:02:55.749
health services spending. So, those are two
financial guardrails that are shaping the

00:02:55.749 --> 00:03:02.419
final stages of producing the NHS long-term
plan. Excellent work has been done on that

00:03:02.419 --> 00:03:09.109
by the many working groups, patients groups,
frontline leaders across the National Health

00:03:09.109 --> 00:03:20.120
Service. The day that it is published will
depend somewhat on timings of other public

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:24.279
business linked to Brexit, but we intend that
it will be published sometime between the

00:03:24.279 --> 00:03:31.699
12th and the 21st of December, and it is pretty
nearly ready to go.

00:03:31.699 --> 00:03:39.299
And then related to that, of course, finally,
I was with Matt Hancock and others giving

00:03:39.299 --> 00:03:46.329
evidence yesterday to Parliament about NHS
and Brexit, and we’ve got a lot of work

00:03:46.329 --> 00:03:52.309
being led by the Department of Health and
Social Care on preparation in the event that

00:03:52.309 --> 00:04:01.650
there is not an agreed deal by 23.00 on March
29th. The particular issue for the NHS arises

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:08.590
in connection with supply chains and transportation
and logistics, so we are advising the Department

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:15.281
of Health and Social Care on those aspects
where, for clinical or other reasons, special

00:04:15.281 --> 00:04:22.280
arrangements will be needed. As I said to
Parliament yesterday, if by Christmas, we

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:27.550
do not have certainty that a no-deal outcome
is going to be avoided, then a number of those

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:32.889
contingency plans will need to be put into
place, and I particularly want to thank our

00:04:32.889 --> 00:04:37.139
pharmacy team and medical directorate for
the work they’re doing with the Department

00:04:37.139 --> 00:04:43.310
of Health and Social Care on that. And working
with NHS Improvement, trusts are completing

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:48.340
their assessment around their supply chain
and contracts by the 30th of November, and

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:53.350
we will then use the first ten days of December
to assess what that means for the contingency

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:57.770
plans that need enacting.
So, those were the three things that I was

00:04:57.770 --> 00:05:03.699
going to report on, David: the Budget, the
long-term plan and Brexit preparation. The

00:05:03.699 --> 00:05:07.750
items are covered on our agenda.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay. Any questions for Simon

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:10.979
on any of those three items? Maybe I could
ask you one question, Simon.

00:05:10.979 --> 00:05:16.550
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: This is an unwelcome departure
from established practice, your predecessor…

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:23.870
Yes, Chairman?
&gt;&gt;David Prior: I’d just be interested to

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:27.870
get your reflections, because you – when
you produced the Five Year Forward View four

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:33.419
years ago now, I think, the sort of – the
difference between how you see the Five Year

00:05:33.419 --> 00:05:37.960
Forward View that you did ten, with the Ten
Year Forward View that you’re doing – I

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:41.180
mean, not just the obvious difference between
five years and ten years, but just in terms

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:48.310
of the journey towards reshaping the way we
deliver healthcare in the UK, how would you

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:51.970
sort of set those two…
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Well, I think there are two

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:56.909
big differences. One is that, at the time
the NHS constructed the Five Year Forward

00:05:56.909 --> 00:06:02.229
View in October 2014, we were obviously coming
out of a period where there’d been a lot

00:06:02.229 --> 00:06:09.389
of time and attention tied up with the 2012
legislation, rather than a focus on actually

00:06:09.389 --> 00:06:15.610
how care needed to change. And so the Forward
View was really a moment to take stock and

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:19.669
say, actually, the NHS is going to have to
look quite different for the following reasons,

00:06:19.669 --> 00:06:26.189
and behave and work in different ways, and
so it sets an agenda as to why change was

00:06:26.189 --> 00:06:31.889
needed. What we’re now doing is getting
much more granular about the what and the

00:06:31.889 --> 00:06:34.370
how.
And the second big difference is that, at

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:41.620
the time the Forward View was drawn up, we
were doing that not knowing what the funding

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:46.909
envelope would look like for the following
five years, whereas we have made our argument

00:06:46.909 --> 00:06:51.139
in connection with the run-up to the 70th
anniversary of the NHS, have got our five-year

00:06:51.139 --> 00:06:55.889
funding settlement, and now, on the back of
that, are able to cycle off accordingly, with

00:06:55.889 --> 00:07:02.940
a couple of caveats, important caveats, particularly
linked to the fact that, obviously, the NHS

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:10.169
long-term plan was never expected to and cannot
answer definitively the question around workforce

00:07:10.169 --> 00:07:15.180
training, because as the Secretary of State
said yesterday at the Health and Social Care

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:18.909
Committee, that’s obviously going to be
the NHE budget going to be set as part of

00:07:18.909 --> 00:07:27.020
the spending review. Ditto for capital and
local-authority public health grants, and

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:30.569
indeed, for adult social care. So, there are
still a number of very important things that

00:07:30.569 --> 00:07:37.620
will have to be got right during the course
of 2019, but in terms of the revenue funding

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:42.889
envelope for the NHS, we have that certainty
now, which we did not have when the Forward

00:07:42.889 --> 00:07:46.389
View was established.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah, okay. Interesting. Moira?

00:07:46.389 --> 00:07:51.189
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: Can I ask, just in that same
reflective mode that you’re in, David, since

00:07:51.189 --> 00:07:55.770
I’m not going to be here at the next Board
meeting, but just to ask Simon how he thinks

00:07:55.770 --> 00:08:02.130
the relationship with social care has developed
over that same period, really, and how he

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:07.870
would describe it now and going forward.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Well, I think in some ways

00:08:07.870 --> 00:08:13.479
similar to the increasing focus on mental
health, the social care debate has come up

00:08:13.479 --> 00:08:18.159
the national agenda, and I think you now can’t
have a conversation about the future sustainability

00:08:18.159 --> 00:08:24.710
of the NHS without also recognising the interdependency
with social care. Some people say that that’s

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:28.770
too reductionist in that social care is not
just about its interface with the NHS, and

00:08:28.770 --> 00:08:37.140
that’s clearly right. But equally, part
of what’s created such salience and urgency

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:43.650
is the fact that people can see that, for
particularly frail older people, without that

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:52.370
home-based support, pressure piles into expensive
and overheated parts of the system, namely,

00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:58.640
acute hospitals, and that in turn means it’s
harder to provide quick emergency care and

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:03.320
to do the planned waiting-list work that people
also want to see. So, I think the kind of

00:09:03.320 --> 00:09:07.930
interconnectedness argument has moved a long
way over the course of that four or five years.

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:14.070
Obviously, what has not moved is a settled
national consensus about a durable future

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:21.631
funding answer for social care services. The
government is intending, I believe, to publish

00:09:21.631 --> 00:09:27.690
a green paper on the same sort of timeline,
so we’re told, as the long-term plan, but

00:09:27.690 --> 00:09:35.300
that will be a green paper.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Good. Any other – any questions?

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:40.950
Okay. Thanks very much, Simon. Emily, freedom
to speak up.

00:09:40.950 --> 00:09:47.130
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: Yes. So, I will – checking
which microphone I should be speaking to.

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:51.380
I will just do a brief summary on the paper
that came second, which is the freedom to

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:56.750
speak up inside NHS England, and then hand
over to Henrietta, who has joined us, at the

00:09:56.750 --> 00:10:01.200
end of the table, from the National Guardian
to talk about the work across the NHS. So,

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:08.220
in NHS England, as discussed at the Board
meeting last year, given our size of organisation,

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:13.570
we are – we should have one freedom to speak
up guardian, at least, and in discussion with

00:10:13.570 --> 00:10:17.720
the organisation, we decided to go much bigger
than that, and to say that we should have

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:22.380
as many broadly as wanted to come forward,
who are appropriately qualified to do the

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:28.350
role. And we made a commitment to do that
last year. We have appointed 40 freedom to

00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:33.630
speak up guardians, and we – in order to
make sure as many locations as possible were

00:10:33.630 --> 00:10:39.290
covered, we split that into lead guardians
and then support guardians, where there was

00:10:39.290 --> 00:10:47.390
a slightly lower time commitment required
and experience required to do the role. We

00:10:47.390 --> 00:10:52.570
also made a commitment that one third of those
would be from a BME background, which we achieved.

00:10:52.570 --> 00:10:58.720
And that network was established by the end
of last year, and we’ve been doing several

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:03.240
things to try and set people up to do that
role effectively. One was obviously making

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:08.480
sure the recruitment process was managed well
and had as much attention in the organisation

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:14.150
as required. We did a lot of training, where
we had great support from the National Guardian’s

00:11:14.150 --> 00:11:17.730
Office to do so.
And then we’ve been asking the network to

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:22.250
regularly feed back about what else they need,
and there have been two things that they’ve

00:11:22.250 --> 00:11:27.190
regularly asked for. One is further guidance
post the training, to support their work,

00:11:27.190 --> 00:11:31.440
to really help them think about how to have
conversations, how best to support colleagues

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:37.580
who come forward. One is to differentiate,
is this a freedom to speak up case or not,

00:11:37.580 --> 00:11:43.191
and to help guide colleagues to the right
support that’s available. And then the second

00:11:43.191 --> 00:11:47.000
area is just making sure there’s appropriate
material for staff about when they should

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:51.790
approach a freedom to speak up guardian, who
they are, how to get hold of them, etc. So,

00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:57.260
we’ve been looking at that during the year.
We have quarterly workshop meetings where

00:11:57.260 --> 00:12:00.820
some people come in person and other people
dial in, so – to review how everybody’s

00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:04.130
doing their role, how they’re finding it
and the kind of cases that are coming up and

00:12:04.130 --> 00:12:08.360
what support’s required. And then there’s
also a call every two weeks for people to

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:12.670
dial into, so they can get immediate help
and support if they need it.

00:12:12.670 --> 00:12:16.721
During the year, we’ve had a few people
change roles and say that they don’t feel

00:12:16.721 --> 00:12:21.310
able to keep playing the role, given their
new responsibilities. As I mentioned earlier,

00:12:21.310 --> 00:12:25.410
we don’t really need to appoint anybody
else just by numbers, but what we’ve found

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:29.270
through having this larger network is that
it’s actually very helpful for colleagues

00:12:29.270 --> 00:12:34.770
to have guardians immediately accessible in
each geography. So, we’re doing – given

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:38.741
we have multiple office locations nationally,
we’ve been – we’re currently doing a

00:12:38.741 --> 00:12:43.690
review to see, should we actually fill some
of those gaps or should we just ask the existing

00:12:43.690 --> 00:12:49.060
network to cover those areas.
One of our commitments last year was to think

00:12:49.060 --> 00:12:53.010
about the freedom to speak up challenges in
primary care, which I know Henrietta’s going

00:12:53.010 --> 00:12:58.820
to say a bit more about in her paper, so I
should just say that the important characteristics

00:12:58.820 --> 00:13:03.550
here are that there isn’t a single appropriate
model for primary care, because there’s

00:13:03.550 --> 00:13:08.070
so many different ways in which this is delivered.
So, one of the critical things here is to

00:13:08.070 --> 00:13:12.170
make sure people have the guidance to help
tailor the freedom to speak up approach and

00:13:12.170 --> 00:13:17.800
experience for colleagues. But what we have
done in the meantime is to work with Health

00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:22.420
Education England to develop an e-learning
programme, which raises awareness of the freedom

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:27.290
to speak up opportunity for staff in primary
care in advance of having the model completely

00:13:27.290 --> 00:13:30.040
worked through, which Henrietta will update
on further.

00:13:30.040 --> 00:13:35.231
So, we’re doing a formal review now to look
at the first 12 months of freedom to speak

00:13:35.231 --> 00:13:40.050
up inside NHS England. We’re giving feedback
to the National Guardian’s Office about

00:13:40.050 --> 00:13:44.950
how training can be further adapted, in particular,
putting practical case studies in outside

00:13:44.950 --> 00:13:49.320
of the provider setting to help people understand
what kind of cases might come up and what

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:53.540
they need to handle, and continuing communications
to make sure colleagues know how to access

00:13:53.540 --> 00:13:58.070
this, because I think that’s probably the
biggest gap right now: some places, very clear;

00:13:58.070 --> 00:14:01.330
other places, less so.
So, that’s the update from NHS England,

00:14:01.330 --> 00:14:04.770
and I wanted to hand over to Henrietta to
talk about the national initiatives.

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:10.190
&gt;&gt;Henrietta Hughes: Thank you very much, Chair.
Since I last attended this Board, there have

00:14:10.190 --> 00:14:15.570
been substantial changes to the speaking up
landscape across England, and we’ve now

00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:20.210
– in addition to the guardians and champions
you have at NHS England, we’ve got over

00:14:20.210 --> 00:14:25.930
800 guardians and champions and ambassadors
across the country in the trusts, in the arm’s-length

00:14:25.930 --> 00:14:32.420
bodies, and in independent sector providers,
and also, in a university. We’ve had over

00:14:32.420 --> 00:14:38.340
12,000 cases raised to date, and the largest
group of those is around bullying and harassment.

00:14:38.340 --> 00:14:43.790
We’ve – as Emily said, we have provided
training and development for guardians, but

00:14:43.790 --> 00:14:48.510
also, in conjunction with NHS Improvement,
board guidance documents, so that boards are

00:14:48.510 --> 00:14:55.410
aware of their responsibilities in this space.
We’ve recently published our 2018 survey,

00:14:55.410 --> 00:15:00.940
and what this shows is that 40% of guardians
don’t have any protected time. And this

00:15:00.940 --> 00:15:08.460
impacts on their ability to do the role justice.
But also, from the results of the perceptions

00:15:08.460 --> 00:15:14.421
of guardians, it appears once again that the
better organisations as rated by the CQC appear

00:15:14.421 --> 00:15:20.570
to do speaking up better. But we want organisations
to use speaking up as a way of improving.

00:15:20.570 --> 00:15:24.610
As they say at West Suffolk, ‘Freedom to
speak up, freedom to improve’, and I think

00:15:24.610 --> 00:15:29.740
that’s a very good motto, to see this as
an opportunity for the ideas, the concerns,

00:15:29.740 --> 00:15:35.930
but also, the issues that staff are facing
on a daily basis, to be brought to the attention

00:15:35.930 --> 00:15:40.260
of the organisation so that positive changes
can be made, not only for the working lives

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:43.030
of staff, but more importantly, for patient
care.

00:15:43.030 --> 00:15:49.170
We’ve recently completed a 12-month pilot
of our case review process, and we’re evaluating

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:55.480
that so that we can develop a system of case
reviews which will be broader, but also more

00:15:55.480 --> 00:16:01.430
rapidly completed. And through the case reviews,
we’re identifying and systematically tackling

00:16:01.430 --> 00:16:06.670
the barriers to speaking up, and some examples
of those are conflicts of interest and conflicts

00:16:06.670 --> 00:16:13.230
of loyalty, the way that settlement agreements
are written, bias in the way that investigations

00:16:13.230 --> 00:16:18.810
are done, and also around bullying and harassment.
So, what we do is, we make the recommendations

00:16:18.810 --> 00:16:24.350
to the trust, but we also expect every trust
in the country to read the reports, identify

00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:29.670
the areas that they can improve on by doing
a gap analysis, and thereby using those recommendations

00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:33.870
to support their own improvement, as well.
And we’ve got some special projects that

00:16:33.870 --> 00:16:38.050
we’re doing in partnership at the moment,
including one on bullying and harassment with

00:16:38.050 --> 00:16:41.360
the Royal College of Surgeons of Edinburgh,
who’ve done some really groundbreaking work

00:16:41.360 --> 00:16:46.040
on this, where we’ve brought together 20
organisations to form an alliance which is

00:16:46.040 --> 00:16:50.840
not only about doctors – this is much broader
than about doctors – but it’s also recognising

00:16:50.840 --> 00:16:55.700
that this is a four-nation issue.
We’re doing some work on settlement agreements

00:16:55.700 --> 00:17:00.450
with NHS Employers, with some law firms, but
also NHS Improvement and the Department of

00:17:00.450 --> 00:17:05.270
Health, so that we can ensure that you don’t
require to have a law degree to understand

00:17:05.270 --> 00:17:09.270
what’s written in your settlement agreement,
because we’ve seen this has been a barrier

00:17:09.270 --> 00:17:14.190
to people being able to raise concerns about
patient safety, because it appears that they’re

00:17:14.190 --> 00:17:20.699
not entitled to do so under the terms of their
agreement. And with NHS England, we’re also

00:17:20.699 --> 00:17:24.450
contributing to the work that you’re doing
about conflicts of interest. In one of the

00:17:24.450 --> 00:17:29.250
case reviews we did, out of a staff of 9,000
in a trust, only one person had completed

00:17:29.250 --> 00:17:33.500
a conflicts of interest declaration, and we
believe there’s substantial improvement

00:17:33.500 --> 00:17:39.490
that can happen across the country, particularly
in trusts where there are lots of relationships

00:17:39.490 --> 00:17:45.690
and undisclosed conflicts of interest which
can affect people’s confidence that they

00:17:45.690 --> 00:17:50.130
will get a fair hearing or that a fair investigation
will be done.

00:17:50.130 --> 00:17:54.930
And as Emily said, our future priorities are
in terms of expanding into primary care. We’re

00:17:54.930 --> 00:18:01.060
already working with some vanguard organisations,
including a local medical committee, a commissioning

00:18:01.060 --> 00:18:07.300
support unit and its associated CCGs, some
large GP providers, and also the defence medical

00:18:07.300 --> 00:18:11.620
services. We want to look at different models
in the way that this can be done, recognising

00:18:11.620 --> 00:18:16.660
that there are different relationships in
different parts of the country, and so realising

00:18:16.660 --> 00:18:20.020
that there won’t be a one-size-fits-all,
but there’ll be some learning that we can

00:18:20.020 --> 00:18:24.270
get from the vanguards. And we’re obviously
adapting the training that we’re doing so

00:18:24.270 --> 00:18:29.260
that it matches the needs of each of those
vanguard organisations. We’re very mindful

00:18:29.260 --> 00:18:34.080
of the changes in the regional structure of
NHS England and NHS Improvement, and we’re

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:40.800
looking to match our regions so that they
mirror your footprint, and to have a regional

00:18:40.800 --> 00:18:45.340
liaison manager in each region to create an
integrated approach to speaking up across

00:18:45.340 --> 00:18:49.810
the whole patient pathway, hopefully trying
to break down some of those organisational

00:18:49.810 --> 00:18:54.450
barriers between primary and secondary care,
community, and potentially into social care,

00:18:54.450 --> 00:19:00.670
as well, so that we can actually start getting
some of those problems resolved. We’re also

00:19:00.670 --> 00:19:05.350
developing phase two of the case review process,
as I mentioned. We’re collecting a library

00:19:05.350 --> 00:19:11.560
of case studies, and I think it’s very valuable
to consider the cases that will be relevant

00:19:11.560 --> 00:19:16.710
to organisations that aren’t providing care.
And so really, my sort of ask of the Board

00:19:16.710 --> 00:19:24.190
is to offer you the annual report for this
year, to be able to seek your feedback on

00:19:24.190 --> 00:19:29.710
that, to thank you for the funding to extend
into primary care, for you to continue role-modelling

00:19:29.710 --> 00:19:37.140
good process by having your own guardians,
and also, just to ask me how we can help you

00:19:37.140 --> 00:19:43.640
get your – the future that you want to see
in terms of internally within NHS England,

00:19:43.640 --> 00:19:46.100
but also your work out into the healthcare
system.

00:19:46.100 --> 00:19:51.140
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you very much, Henrietta.
How do we know – or how do you know – that

00:19:51.140 --> 00:19:54.630
you’re making a difference?
&gt;&gt;Henrietta Hughes: Well, I think there are

00:19:54.630 --> 00:19:58.480
– the feedback from the individuals who’ve
been supported by guardians is probably the

00:19:58.480 --> 00:20:05.920
most important thing, and very much, considering
the aspects of our role, is the listening

00:20:05.920 --> 00:20:10.780
to individuals. So, from the case reviews,
from the interactions they have with guardians,

00:20:10.780 --> 00:20:15.000
it’s not uncommon for people to say, ‘This
is the first time I’ve felt truly listened

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:20.650
to’. So, I’m looking at it from the sort
of micro on the effect on the individual.

00:20:20.650 --> 00:20:25.820
And then when we look at the effect that it
has on organisations, so when people are reading

00:20:25.820 --> 00:20:30.600
the case reviews and immediately making improvements
to their policies, to their processes, but

00:20:30.600 --> 00:20:35.360
also, the way that they interact with their
staff, and are genuinely interested in listening

00:20:35.360 --> 00:20:38.980
to the views of their staff and using that
to develop their strategy.

00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:44.570
And then at a wider level across the NHS,
I think this has gone from being an initiative,

00:20:44.570 --> 00:20:49.760
to being quite normalised now, that if you
go into a trust, it’s not something that

00:20:49.760 --> 00:20:56.520
is peculiar, that people know who their guardian
is. As I go and give talks, I will ask the

00:20:56.520 --> 00:20:59.380
audience, ‘Do you know who your freedom
to speak up guardian – have you heard of

00:20:59.380 --> 00:21:04.290
this before?’ And I’ve seen probably an
improvement in that from about 20% of hands

00:21:04.290 --> 00:21:08.120
going up to now, over 50%, and sometimes even
more than that.

00:21:08.120 --> 00:21:13.910
We’re also looking at it from the reverse
side of it, as well, which is that organisations

00:21:13.910 --> 00:21:19.390
that haven’t had any cases raised, or who
haven’t sent us data for quite a long period

00:21:19.390 --> 00:21:25.520
of time, have thought very much about what’s
not working well, and have adapted their system

00:21:25.520 --> 00:21:29.810
so that they get the right person in the role
or people in the role, as guardian, and then

00:21:29.810 --> 00:21:34.330
they start seeing cases coming through. So,
I think it’s early days. When I talk to

00:21:34.330 --> 00:21:38.320
people in the airline industry, they say they’ve
been doing this for over 40 years, so two

00:21:38.320 --> 00:21:43.230
years down the line, they wouldn’t be expecting
to see much. But it feels different, to me,

00:21:43.230 --> 00:21:48.150
when I go to visit trusts, talk to staff,
and actually see the benefits that they’re

00:21:48.150 --> 00:21:52.040
getting from having the guardians.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Henrietta. Moira?

00:21:52.040 --> 00:21:55.840
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: I was just going to say, I think
that Henrietta and her team, and the guardians

00:21:55.840 --> 00:22:01.000
themselves, face the challenge that the NHS
faces generally of, how do you get good, positive

00:22:01.000 --> 00:22:08.070
developments replicated across the piece?
It’s not special to guardians. But I’ve

00:22:08.070 --> 00:22:13.241
been, on behalf of NHS England, a member of
the Accountability and Liaison Board, which

00:22:13.241 --> 00:22:22.850
is the sort of governance that’s been established
for Henrietta and her colleagues. And I think

00:22:22.850 --> 00:22:28.580
the progress that’s been made means that
this is the right time to review that governance,

00:22:28.580 --> 00:22:34.230
particularly given that NHSI and E are coming
much closer today, and a board of two rather

00:22:34.230 --> 00:22:41.190
than three would feel distinctly odd. And
I think there is a question about whether

00:22:41.190 --> 00:22:46.690
it should continue to be non-execs or could
become more appropriately an executive function.

00:22:46.690 --> 00:22:51.370
But I think that – this is the time to do
that, not just that I’m going.

00:22:51.370 --> 00:22:57.640
&gt;&gt;David Prior: David?
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: So, first of all, I think

00:22:57.640 --> 00:22:58.640
both to Henrietta and to Emily, great progress
in the last year since you were here last

00:22:58.640 --> 00:23:03.230
time, so thank you for that. I guess my question
is that freedom to speak up needs to be matched

00:23:03.230 --> 00:23:09.990
by willingness to listen and act, and therefore,
both on an individual basis, but also, on

00:23:09.990 --> 00:23:14.670
a systemic and learning basis. So, I’d be
intrigued to hear from both of you, both within

00:23:14.670 --> 00:23:20.410
NHS England, but also more across the health
sector, whether you think there is a willingness

00:23:20.410 --> 00:23:26.460
to listen and act that’s coming in, or whether
there are still barriers to people sort of

00:23:26.460 --> 00:23:29.120
accepting that things have gone wrong and
need to be addressed.

00:23:29.120 --> 00:23:31.240
&gt;&gt;Henrietta Hughes: I’m happy to start off.
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Okay.

00:23:31.240 --> 00:23:35.640
&gt;&gt;Henrietta Hughes: I would absolutely agree
with you that the freedom to speak up is when

00:23:35.640 --> 00:23:41.520
leaders and managers listen. That is the freedom,
and obviously, it’s a slightly unusual title

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:46.870
to have, and I’ve done quite a lot of thinking
about what it means. So, I think that’s

00:23:46.870 --> 00:23:52.530
variable, and it can be variable between organisations,
within organisations, even within teams. I

00:23:52.530 --> 00:23:56.960
take the view that we want to have a growth
mindset, so there’s something about, you

00:23:56.960 --> 00:24:01.010
can’t fix everything that you call out,
but until you call it out, you can’t fix

00:24:01.010 --> 00:24:02.010
it.
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Correct.

00:24:02.010 --> 00:24:06.020
&gt;&gt;Henrietta Hughes: So, part of it is developing
environments of psychological safety, where

00:24:06.020 --> 00:24:11.040
not only people feel able to speak up, but
managers have got the time and the capacity,

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:16.390
and the confidence, to be able to hear those
things. So, I would say that goes all the

00:24:16.390 --> 00:24:22.490
way up through the NHS, into the commissioners,
the regulators, the inspectors, and part of

00:24:22.490 --> 00:24:28.880
that is having boards who’ve got the mindset
that they really want to know what’s happening,

00:24:28.880 --> 00:24:34.470
but then translating that into training and
time and leadership, into their middle managers,

00:24:34.470 --> 00:24:39.790
as well. So, we’ve got to support middle
managers to have the time and to have the

00:24:39.790 --> 00:24:46.290
skills, and to feel that there is room within
what they’re expecting to manage, to be

00:24:46.290 --> 00:24:51.280
able to hear the ideas and the concerns from
their teams. And if we can translate that

00:24:51.280 --> 00:24:54.200
all the way back down to the workforce, then
that gives them that freedom.

00:24:54.200 --> 00:24:57.600
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: And I think this issue of
the psychological safety, both to acknowledge

00:24:57.600 --> 00:25:04.050
that things haven’t worked, and to see it
as an opportunity to improve without getting

00:25:04.050 --> 00:25:09.720
sort of dumped on from on high, which is what
often can happen, because that’s what the

00:25:09.720 --> 00:25:14.450
disconnect between, if you like, the behaviour
as to how people have responded when they’re

00:25:14.450 --> 00:25:19.830
trying to do something to improve, recognising
that things under their watch have gone wrong,

00:25:19.830 --> 00:25:24.250
or not as well as they could have done, I
think is a real responsibility for boards

00:25:24.250 --> 00:25:28.790
to set that tone. In my industry, financial
services, that’s been a big conversation

00:25:28.790 --> 00:25:34.360
over the last sort of five to ten years. Still,
I think, room to improve, and I’d encourage

00:25:34.360 --> 00:25:40.850
you to think really sort of hard and quite
forcefully about making sure that boards recognise

00:25:40.850 --> 00:25:48.130
that they have to lead by how they react when
things go wrong, not just about sort of recognising

00:25:48.130 --> 00:25:51.820
about how we encourage the freedom to actually
raise issues.

00:25:51.820 --> 00:25:55.360
&gt;&gt;Henrietta Hughes: Yeah, and we do have a
pan-sector network, because we recognise that

00:25:55.360 --> 00:25:59.060
even if we look at the very best in the NHS,
there will still be more that we can learn

00:25:59.060 --> 00:26:03.460
from other sectors. So, in the pan-sector
network, we have representatives from the

00:26:03.460 --> 00:26:11.180
Army, from Acas, from banks, finance, from
aviation, but also from sport, from regulators.

00:26:11.180 --> 00:26:14.970
And everybody’s struggling with the same
thing. So, there’s no – nobody’s got

00:26:14.970 --> 00:26:19.360
this perfect, but there’s so much that we
can learn from the way that other sectors

00:26:19.360 --> 00:26:25.090
approach this. And also, in terms of the support
and the training that they give to their workforce,

00:26:25.090 --> 00:26:31.310
which I think is absolutely key.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: And Emily then – sorry, Emily

00:26:31.310 --> 00:26:32.630
then Victor.
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: Yeah, I’d obviously agree

00:26:32.630 --> 00:26:36.010
with everything Henrietta’s said on that.
As Henrietta and I have discussed, I think

00:26:36.010 --> 00:26:39.850
to some extent, the need to have a freedom
to speak up guardian indicates that we don’t

00:26:39.850 --> 00:26:43.320
have the listening culture in the first place,
right? Because if everybody was open to learning,

00:26:43.320 --> 00:26:47.651
we wouldn’t need a separate way for people
to go. If I look at the eight cases that have

00:26:47.651 --> 00:26:54.380
been raised inside NHS England through freedom
to speak up guardians this year, more than

00:26:54.380 --> 00:26:58.860
one of them – but I won’t commit to exactly
how many – didn’t need – wouldn’t

00:26:58.860 --> 00:27:03.330
have needed to have been a freedom to speak
up issue, had earlier conversations been had

00:27:03.330 --> 00:27:06.230
in the right kind of listening way. So, there’s
obviously more we can do.

00:27:06.230 --> 00:27:10.490
What we have done is some of what Henrietta’s
talking about, is, we have the line manager

00:27:10.490 --> 00:27:14.711
development programme, which about a quarter
of the organisation are eligible for, and

00:27:14.711 --> 00:27:18.380
some of whom have already done. And then – now
we have the senior line manager development

00:27:18.380 --> 00:27:22.820
programme, which has only had one and a half
cohorts so far, of 30 people each. We’ve

00:27:22.820 --> 00:27:27.980
built in this whole area of how to have these
kind of conversations, how to listen without

00:27:27.980 --> 00:27:32.470
feeling like you’re taking all of the blame.
I’m not going to say that is a magic bullet,

00:27:32.470 --> 00:27:36.690
but it’s sort of one line that at least
starts people’s awareness of how they need

00:27:36.690 --> 00:27:40.340
to respond when these kind of things come
up. I take your point that it’s probably

00:27:40.340 --> 00:27:45.570
something we can look at again as an executive
group, and it is something we are discussing

00:27:45.570 --> 00:27:49.910
with NHS Improvement, because obviously, given
the work they already do, they have – we

00:27:49.910 --> 00:27:54.740
need to be consistent, but equally, there
are sort of abilities and approaches they’ve

00:27:54.740 --> 00:27:57.710
got that we could learn from and build into
how we approach this, as well.

00:27:57.710 --> 00:28:00.020
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Victor?
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Two things. The first

00:28:00.020 --> 00:28:03.960
is, I mean, this is good stuff. I mean, how
can you say anything else? It is good stuff.

00:28:03.960 --> 00:28:09.900
The question – is this notion of how you
– this is about developing a learning culture,

00:28:09.900 --> 00:28:15.140
isn’t it, basically? It’s about learning.
And so I am curious about how we are – whether

00:28:15.140 --> 00:28:20.410
we have a whole-system approach to this with
our – the leadership academy, workforce

00:28:20.410 --> 00:28:24.980
race equality strategy. Just how – could
you say a bit more about how we’re approaching

00:28:24.980 --> 00:28:27.681
the whole system? You’ve mentioned some
training stuff, but I just think that’s

00:28:27.681 --> 00:28:34.060
the – including people around this table,
fish rots from the head and all that stuff.

00:28:34.060 --> 00:28:38.590
And the other is, I just wonder, what, in
a year’s time – because I know it’s

00:28:38.590 --> 00:28:44.670
early days, and these things take time – but
what signals are you looking for that we’re

00:28:44.670 --> 00:28:50.330
actually moving in the right direction? What
kind of culture – what shifts are you looking

00:28:50.330 --> 00:28:55.600
for in the first year? Because of course,
this is an – it’s an initiative because

00:28:55.600 --> 00:29:00.370
we’re failing to do something, so I’m
just interested in what shift are we looking

00:29:00.370 --> 00:29:05.020
to see in the first year that might indicate
that we’re doing the right – it’s the

00:29:05.020 --> 00:29:08.429
right intervention? What systemic shift?
&gt;&gt;Henrietta Hughes: I’d like to see changes

00:29:08.429 --> 00:29:12.290
quicker than a year. I think every trust should
have ringfenced time for their freedom to

00:29:12.290 --> 00:29:17.530
speak up guardian. I think the idea that they’re
able to do this valuable and important work

00:29:17.530 --> 00:29:22.560
on fresh air and goodwill is – doesn’t
do justice to their staff, it doesn’t suggest

00:29:22.560 --> 00:29:28.950
the right level of investment into the workforce.
And we’ve seen the knock-on effect of people

00:29:28.950 --> 00:29:32.510
not having ringfenced time, is that they don’t
present to their board in person; the boards

00:29:32.510 --> 00:29:36.500
need to be meeting the guardians to ask them
the right questions. They don’t turn up

00:29:36.500 --> 00:29:40.540
to training or to regional meetings where
they’d get the support and the learning

00:29:40.540 --> 00:29:46.660
from their colleagues. And probably most importantly,
they don’t seek feedback on their performance,

00:29:46.660 --> 00:29:50.440
and it’s only that very first question I
was asked: how do we know that this is working?

00:29:50.440 --> 00:29:55.500
We only know it’s working if people are
actually asked for their experience of, how

00:29:55.500 --> 00:29:59.059
was it for you? Would you do this again? So,
I think that’s the thing I would be looking

00:29:59.059 --> 00:30:02.970
for very quickly. And then when boards are
actually meeting their guardians and being

00:30:02.970 --> 00:30:06.290
able to ask them the right questions, they’ll
then understand what’s happening in their

00:30:06.290 --> 00:30:10.530
own organisation. So, for me, that would be
the sine qua non first step.

00:30:10.530 --> 00:30:14.970
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: And the workforce race
equality strategy, how’s that?

00:30:14.970 --> 00:30:19.600
&gt;&gt;Henrietta Hughes: Well, in trusts, we expect
guardians to work in partnership with the

00:30:19.600 --> 00:30:24.000
res team, with complaints and incidents, with
staff side, occupational health, with the

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:27.920
guardian of safe working, so that they can
start learning about the hotspots of culture

00:30:27.920 --> 00:30:33.140
in their own trusts. So, we see that as absolutely
key, and we do model that similarly at national

00:30:33.140 --> 00:30:36.280
level.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Richard, then I think we’d

00:30:36.280 --> 00:30:40.610
better move on. Richard?
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: One really quick one, because

00:30:40.610 --> 00:30:43.960
it worries me that I may be being very naïve
on this, Chair, but it’s the issue that

00:30:43.960 --> 00:30:49.950
Henrietta raised about settlement letters.
And I thought we’d moved beyond settlement

00:30:49.950 --> 00:30:55.390
letters in any way preventing people from
speaking up on patient safety issues, so I

00:30:55.390 --> 00:31:01.640
was surprised that was still there as an issue.
Have I misunderstood what you’ve said, or

00:31:01.640 --> 00:31:07.500
have I been incredibly naïve?
&gt;&gt;Henrietta Hughes: So, my experience is that

00:31:07.500 --> 00:31:12.679
the way that they’re written can be so opaque,
is that it doesn’t make it clear. And also,

00:31:12.679 --> 00:31:17.840
if the existence of the settlement agreement
in itself is confidential, then that can inhibit

00:31:17.840 --> 00:31:21.760
people from approaching, for example, the
whistleblower support scheme, from joining

00:31:21.760 --> 00:31:27.730
my advisory working group. But there’s something
about the way the wording is written, that

00:31:27.730 --> 00:31:32.950
if it says that under section 43J of the ERA,
your rights are fully established, not everybody

00:31:32.950 --> 00:31:36.549
understands what that means. And on another
part of the document, there may be a whole

00:31:36.549 --> 00:31:40.420
paragraph saying you can’t say anything
about circumstances leading up to the termination

00:31:40.420 --> 00:31:45.790
of your employment. So, it’s confusing,
and we’re working to clarify that so that

00:31:45.790 --> 00:31:49.540
anyone who signs a settlement agreement, it
is absolutely crystal clear what their rights

00:31:49.540 --> 00:31:51.500
are.
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: So, rather than lots of

00:31:51.500 --> 00:31:54.380
lawyers, don’t you just need to say every
settlement letter needs to have a sentence

00:31:54.380 --> 00:31:59.210
in that says, ‘None of this prevents you
speaking up on patient safety issues’?

00:31:59.210 --> 00:32:00.500
&gt;&gt;Henrietta Hughes: Precisely.
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: It sort of feels quite

00:32:00.500 --> 00:32:01.770
simple.
&gt;&gt;Henrietta Hughes: Precisely.

00:32:01.770 --> 00:32:05.919
&gt;&gt;David Prior: And if it’s as simple as
that, can’t we do that?

00:32:05.919 --> 00:32:09.150
&gt;&gt;Henrietta Hughes: That’s what we’re
doing through NHS Employers. We’re providing

00:32:09.150 --> 00:32:12.090
additional information to go along with their
new guidance.

00:32:12.090 --> 00:32:14.450
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay.
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: Just on a mechanism that

00:32:14.450 --> 00:32:17.310
might help, Henrietta, on that – and we
can take this offline – but I mean, it’s

00:32:17.310 --> 00:32:25.679
possible we could introduce through the standard
contract a requirement which would say that

00:32:25.679 --> 00:32:28.780
those clear guarantees have to be included
in any such process, and we have begun to

00:32:28.780 --> 00:32:35.330
use the NHS standard contract for a variety
of what have traditionally been off-limits

00:32:35.330 --> 00:32:40.990
HR-type topics, including obviously repayment
of redundancies, other matters. So, let’s

00:32:40.990 --> 00:32:44.630
work with you on that and see if we can get
that sorted.

00:32:44.630 --> 00:32:45.630
&gt;&gt;Henrietta Hughes: Sounds wonderful, thank
you.

00:32:45.630 --> 00:32:47.760
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay. Henrietta, thanks very
much. You mentioned in I think West Suffolk,

00:32:47.760 --> 00:32:51.320
you said, have changed from freedom to speak
up to freedom to improve, I think you mentioned.

00:32:51.320 --> 00:32:52.980
&gt;&gt;Henrietta Hughes: They did that even before
the Francis Report.

00:32:52.980 --> 00:32:55.740
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay. Which is a really nice
way of doing it, going back to the learning

00:32:55.740 --> 00:32:58.510
culture that Victor was talking about. I mean,
maybe – is it Steve Dunn who’s the chief

00:32:58.510 --> 00:33:04.230
executive there? I mean, maybe we might ask
him to come down and tell us what he’s done.

00:33:04.230 --> 00:33:08.210
Or not necessarily him, but there maybe someone
else, but he would be probably quite good

00:33:08.210 --> 00:33:10.799
to do that.
&gt;&gt;Henrietta Hughes: I think it’s one example

00:33:10.799 --> 00:33:14.230
of many great examples that I’ve seen across
the country, where this is really affecting

00:33:14.230 --> 00:33:17.630
the staff. So…
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay. Well, it would be good

00:33:17.630 --> 00:33:21.040
to hear from them as well, at some point.
Thank you very much.

00:33:21.040 --> 00:33:22.910
&gt;&gt;Henrietta Hughes: Thank you.
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: Thank you.

00:33:22.910 --> 00:33:24.120
&gt;&gt;David Prior: And thanks, Emily. Thanks very
much. Steve?

00:33:24.120 --> 00:33:29.800
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: Thank you, David. But before
I start, and of course, with your permission,

00:33:29.800 --> 00:33:36.830
I’d like to invite Professor Carrie MacEwen
to join me at the table. Carrie is Chair of

00:33:36.830 --> 00:33:42.100
the Academy of Medical Royal Colleges, and
the Academy is one of our key partners in

00:33:42.100 --> 00:33:45.110
developing this policy. So, welcome, Carrie.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Hello, Carrie. Welcome.

00:33:45.110 --> 00:33:47.730
&gt;&gt;Carrie MacEwen: Thank you.
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: So, the Board will remember

00:33:47.730 --> 00:33:55.030
that in July, I presented a consultation paper
that set out proposals to tackle the inequitable

00:33:55.030 --> 00:33:58.960
provision of inappropriate interventions across
the country, and I’m sure the Board will

00:33:58.960 --> 00:34:02.590
agree that where there is evidence of clinical
and cost effectiveness, we should endeavour

00:34:02.590 --> 00:34:06.950
to introduce new treatment to the clinical
practice as quickly as possible. But it’s

00:34:06.950 --> 00:34:11.520
just as important that we discontinue treatments
and interventions where evidence suggests

00:34:11.520 --> 00:34:14.559
that these treatments are inappropriate or
ineffective.

00:34:14.559 --> 00:34:19.649
And there a number of reasons for doing this,
of which I highlight three again. So, first

00:34:19.649 --> 00:34:25.490
and foremost, we should not offer treatments
that at best disappoint or inconvenience our

00:34:25.490 --> 00:34:31.099
patients, and at the very worse, potentially
harm them. Secondly, we should ensure that

00:34:31.099 --> 00:34:35.419
we deploy our most precious resource – that’s
our staff – to activities that are of proven

00:34:35.419 --> 00:34:40.279
clinical benefit. And third, of course, we
have a responsibility to use taxpayers’

00:34:40.279 --> 00:34:43.710
money wisely.
So, with the Board’s approval, we consulted

00:34:43.710 --> 00:34:48.329
on the evidence-based intervention programme
from the 4th of July to the 28th of September

00:34:48.329 --> 00:34:53.730
this year. And during that consultation period,
we spoke to nearly 400 people at various events

00:34:53.730 --> 00:34:59.999
and workshops, and we received over 700 online
responses and almost 100 email responses to

00:34:59.999 --> 00:35:06.009
our consultation questionnaire. So, we’re
very grateful to all those who’ve contributed,

00:35:06.009 --> 00:35:09.560
because we have absolutely benefited from
a rich array of suggestions and insights,

00:35:09.560 --> 00:35:15.369
from patients, members of the public, clinicians,
specialist societies, and national representative

00:35:15.369 --> 00:35:20.069
bodies and commissioners.
In response to that feedback, we’ve now

00:35:20.069 --> 00:35:23.829
made a number of changes to our proposals,
and I’d like to highlight the following

00:35:23.829 --> 00:35:30.059
three to the Board today. First, we’ve refocused
the programme’s five goals to ensure we

00:35:30.059 --> 00:35:35.010
focus on the appropriate use of interventions.
We’ve also strengthened our collaborative

00:35:35.010 --> 00:35:42.010
approach by establishing a new national steering
group that includes patient and clinical representation.

00:35:42.010 --> 00:35:46.539
This group has helped shape our response,
and will guide our implementation. We’ve

00:35:46.539 --> 00:35:49.880
also refined the messaging, to ensure that
what we’re aiming to do is to ensure that

00:35:49.880 --> 00:35:55.619
interventions in the NHS – that the NHS
offers are appropriate and effective.

00:35:55.619 --> 00:36:01.280
Second, we have revised our clinical criteria
for the 17 interventions, following extensive

00:36:01.280 --> 00:36:05.790
consultation with clinical specialists and
CCGs, to ensure that they are based on the

00:36:05.790 --> 00:36:12.489
clinical evidence developed by NICE, NICE-accredited
or specialist-society guidance. As examples,

00:36:12.489 --> 00:36:18.510
these include expanding the recommendation
wording for carpal tunnel syndrome, Dupuytren’s

00:36:18.510 --> 00:36:22.859
contracture release, ganglion excision and
trigger finger release, to align with proposals

00:36:22.859 --> 00:36:25.869
we received from the British Society of the
Hand.

00:36:25.869 --> 00:36:30.960
Secondly, to exclude children from the criteria
related to Dupuytren’s contracture release,

00:36:30.960 --> 00:36:35.369
trigger finger release and snoring surgery,
as these conditions present differently in

00:36:35.369 --> 00:36:39.430
children and may indicate more serious underlying
conditions.

00:36:39.430 --> 00:36:44.020
Thirdly, we’ve clarified that children who
cannot undergo normal assessments are still

00:36:44.020 --> 00:36:49.239
able to access specialist advice for glue
ear. And finally, accounting for a wide range

00:36:49.239 --> 00:36:54.569
of views on coding methodology, testing this
with coding experts, clinicians and demonstrator

00:36:54.569 --> 00:36:58.599
sites, we’re updating the activity projections
accordingly.

00:36:58.599 --> 00:37:04.600
So, we’ve also worked with commissioners
and providers to test our proposals, and we

00:37:04.600 --> 00:37:09.880
are setting up a number of – a demonstrator
community to continue to refine our proposals

00:37:09.880 --> 00:37:15.319
and monitor implementation. Our work with
these sites is including testing our activity

00:37:15.319 --> 00:37:20.660
reduction figures, which we refined in light
of changes to the clinical criteria and changes

00:37:20.660 --> 00:37:26.190
to our ambition; refining our approach to
accessing the procedures – so there are

00:37:26.190 --> 00:37:30.599
four interventions which will require a successful
individual funding request, and this will

00:37:30.599 --> 00:37:36.380
be backed up by changes to the national tariff
and NHS standard contract. And for the remaining

00:37:36.380 --> 00:37:42.369
13 interventions, we will leave it to CCG
discretion to determine how to achieve the

00:37:42.369 --> 00:37:46.750
goals. However, we would encourage a prior
approval process where there are concerns.

00:37:46.750 --> 00:37:51.759
And finally, clarifying that through the financial
and contractual changes, that these will start

00:37:51.759 --> 00:37:56.509
– although these will start from April 2019,
our expectation is that commissioners, providers

00:37:56.509 --> 00:38:01.410
and clinicians will start to implement the
clinical criteria from today. The rationale

00:38:01.410 --> 00:38:05.700
for this is that we want to ensure patients
have access to the most appropriate interventions

00:38:05.700 --> 00:38:11.420
as possible, and to minimise avoidable harm
to patients as rapidly as possible.

00:38:11.420 --> 00:38:15.890
Lastly, I’d like to make two additional
points. I think it’s really important to

00:38:15.890 --> 00:38:20.940
reiterate that we are not proposing a ban
on any of these procedures. Instead, we’re

00:38:20.940 --> 00:38:26.340
recommending that specific clinical criteria
are met before they are undertaken. All 17

00:38:26.340 --> 00:38:30.930
interventions will still be available in certain
situations according to criteria that we’ve

00:38:30.930 --> 00:38:36.529
outlined in our guidance. I must emphasise,
again, we’ve based these criteria on NICE

00:38:36.529 --> 00:38:41.729
guidance, and worked with clinical experts
to get them right for our patients. And secondly,

00:38:41.729 --> 00:38:46.650
because we think national consistency is important,
we are proposing to issue our clinical criteria

00:38:46.650 --> 00:38:51.529
as statutory guidance.
I’m therefore going to ask the Board to

00:38:51.529 --> 00:38:56.539
approve the policy documents, and furthermore,
to approve the publication of the clinical

00:38:56.539 --> 00:39:04.160
criteria as statutory guidance. I’d like
to thank on behalf of myself and Ian Dodge,

00:39:04.160 --> 00:39:09.109
who’s the other senior sponsor of this programme,
to personally thank everybody who’s contributed

00:39:09.109 --> 00:39:16.220
to the programme: public, patients, commissioners,
specialist societies. And finally, I’d like

00:39:16.220 --> 00:39:21.420
to note our intention to continue this work,
and to return to the Board at a future Board

00:39:21.420 --> 00:39:25.479
meeting with further proposals.
Before I hand back to you for discussion,

00:39:25.479 --> 00:39:30.910
I’d like to ask Ian if he has any further
comments, and then Carrie for comments from

00:39:30.910 --> 00:39:35.239
our – one of our key partners, the Academy.
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Carrie, do you want to…

00:39:35.239 --> 00:39:38.769
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Well, Carrie, thank you very
much for coming today, and for all the work

00:39:38.769 --> 00:39:43.510
that you do and your fellow presidents of
the colleges have put into this. It’s incredibly

00:39:43.510 --> 00:39:46.619
helpful for us, so have you got any comments
you’d like to make?

00:39:46.619 --> 00:39:51.130
&gt;&gt;Carrie MacEwen: The only comment I would
like to add to that is that we – well, one,

00:39:51.130 --> 00:39:55.420
we have been very pleased to be involved,
in that it reflects a lot of the background

00:39:55.420 --> 00:40:01.079
that we’ve been working towards to reduce
waste and harm to patients due to overmedicalisations,

00:40:01.079 --> 00:40:08.559
which we all recognise is out there and happening;
up to 20% of investigations and interventions

00:40:08.559 --> 00:40:12.229
may not be benefiting patients. So, we’re
looking at that very seriously, and this fits

00:40:12.229 --> 00:40:18.950
very well particularly along with the Choosing
Wisely campaign that we’re involved in.

00:40:18.950 --> 00:40:23.729
The other thing that is – is variation,
that we have identified that there is considerable

00:40:23.729 --> 00:40:29.279
variation in many things, but particularly
these groups here. And again, that fits in

00:40:29.279 --> 00:40:35.029
very well with how – the evidence base,
how we’d like to see things working. And

00:40:35.029 --> 00:40:41.380
in the past, what’s happened with medicine
generally is that interventions and investigations

00:40:41.380 --> 00:40:46.180
go in and out depending on the maturity of
the clinicians, largely, and their skills,

00:40:46.180 --> 00:40:50.509
whereas now, what we’re trying to do I think
is manage that better, which is inevitably

00:40:50.509 --> 00:40:55.940
going to be better for patients. So, I think
that that, along with our drive for shared

00:40:55.940 --> 00:41:00.359
decision-making, it’s been very clear that
there has been no suggestion anything’s

00:41:00.359 --> 00:41:05.059
going to be banned, but what this is hoping
to do is to encourage patients and families

00:41:05.059 --> 00:41:09.930
to ask more questions as to why they may be
getting an investigation or a treatment, and

00:41:09.930 --> 00:41:12.730
something else might be better for them, and
they are better involved in that process.

00:41:12.730 --> 00:41:17.299
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Do you see a lot of variation
in your role where you are, a lot of variation

00:41:17.299 --> 00:41:23.469
in medical practice around the country? Yeah?
&gt;&gt;Carrie MacEwen: Yes, there is. And that’s

00:41:23.469 --> 00:41:27.339
one of the reasons, in fact, that the college
has bought in so readily to the Getting It

00:41:27.339 --> 00:41:32.930
Right First Time project, which is looking
at that, and so specialists can go into different

00:41:32.930 --> 00:41:39.789
units and identify why that might be, and
try and help to reduce that. And again, with

00:41:39.789 --> 00:41:46.380
this – with the project in mind here, there
was suggestion that some CCGs were being more

00:41:46.380 --> 00:41:53.069
draconian than others, in that they were already
harder than we were being suggested on the

00:41:53.069 --> 00:41:57.710
evidence base, and really want to raise them,
so they’re not – they’re using the evidence

00:41:57.710 --> 00:42:02.729
base appropriately. So, it’s not just a
clampdown; it’s an improvement to try and,

00:42:02.729 --> 00:42:04.910
again, reduce that variation which we’re
very concerned about.

00:42:04.910 --> 00:42:09.900
&gt;&gt;David Prior: And have you found that clinicians
have been supportive of this programme, on

00:42:09.900 --> 00:42:11.599
balance? I mean, obviously, some people will
have had reservations about it, but on the

00:42:11.599 --> 00:42:17.769
whole, do you find that the professions have
been really driving this?

00:42:17.769 --> 00:42:22.369
&gt;&gt;Carrie MacEwen: Philosophically, yes. I
think it’s always very difficult to change

00:42:22.369 --> 00:42:27.710
practice of an individual, and also of a unit,
and I think that that’s a challenge that

00:42:27.710 --> 00:42:31.559
people do recognise.
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: We’ve had a lot of engagement

00:42:31.559 --> 00:42:35.530
with our professional colleagues, particularly
through the College and through our – the

00:42:35.530 --> 00:42:40.460
various specialist societies. And I think
as I said, they have been very beneficial

00:42:40.460 --> 00:42:46.579
discussions, and have certainly allowed us
to come to a common understanding of the evidence

00:42:46.579 --> 00:42:51.259
base. And that – those discussions are reflected
in our final guidance.

00:42:51.259 --> 00:42:59.789
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Any questions? Noel?
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: I’m very supportive, Steve,

00:42:59.789 --> 00:43:03.380
of the policy framework, and I think it’s
suitably nuanced, as well, particularly with

00:43:03.380 --> 00:43:11.820
cat 2 and access to IFR in exception cases.
But I’m intrigued to know in the consultation

00:43:11.820 --> 00:43:17.839
process how you took account of some of the
patient views, because it comes out quite

00:43:17.839 --> 00:43:25.730
strongly that clinicians are very supportive,
but patients less so. Did the patient voice

00:43:25.730 --> 00:43:30.150
get reflected adequately in the structure
of the policy framework, in your view?

00:43:30.150 --> 00:43:35.359
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: Yes, I think it did. And what
we were clear, in terms of the – we were

00:43:35.359 --> 00:43:39.289
looking for a number of things in the consultation,
asked a number of questions. But really, it

00:43:39.289 --> 00:43:46.170
comes back to the evidence base, and consistently,
what we were looking predominantly was to

00:43:46.170 --> 00:43:51.660
test whether the evidence base or our interpretation
of the evidence base was correct. Now, I think

00:43:51.660 --> 00:44:00.130
by necessity, the comments on the evidence
base came disproportionately from specialist

00:44:00.130 --> 00:44:06.739
societies, colleges, experts in the field.
I think there’s an inevitability about that.

00:44:06.739 --> 00:44:12.339
But I think in terms of weighting input into
our reconsideration of the evidence base,

00:44:12.339 --> 00:44:17.160
we treated all input equally.
I don’t know, Ian, whether you want to add

00:44:17.160 --> 00:44:20.220
to that?
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Yes, so if I could add, I think

00:44:20.220 --> 00:44:25.549
there was a distinction between the responses
of the national patient representative organisations,

00:44:25.549 --> 00:44:31.549
as well as some of the individual patient
responders. And so the national organisations

00:44:31.549 --> 00:44:37.339
were more supportive than some of the individual
responses we had back. Secondly, I think we

00:44:37.339 --> 00:44:45.279
found during the engagement processes, consultation
events, actually, that having the dialogue

00:44:45.279 --> 00:44:51.589
with people in the room really helped to dispel
lots of the myths and concerns that existed.

00:44:51.589 --> 00:44:58.900
And thirdly, we know that a number of the
individual patient responses were partly because

00:44:58.900 --> 00:45:06.650
of a concern either that there was a blanket
ban, or that we were actually intending to,

00:45:06.650 --> 00:45:12.210
you know, radically stop the number of procedures
in a way that wasn’t evidenced by the clinical

00:45:12.210 --> 00:45:17.809
criteria that we’ve actually established,
or that this was about cuts to NHS funding.

00:45:17.809 --> 00:45:24.730
And actually, instead, what we’re really
trying to do here is to free up scarce professional

00:45:24.730 --> 00:45:29.440
time, create headroom for innovation, as well
as perform other interventions. And I think

00:45:29.440 --> 00:45:35.229
what it partly emphasises is the need for
us to continue to plug away with really effective

00:45:35.229 --> 00:45:38.010
communications, including with those patient
groups.

00:45:38.010 --> 00:45:44.420
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Excellent. Well, it’s a great
bit of work, Steve. I think we’re all wholly

00:45:44.420 --> 00:45:47.539
in favour of it, aren’t we? Yeah? Yeah,
Carrie, many thanks to you and all your members

00:45:47.539 --> 00:45:49.269
for contributing to it, as well.
&gt;&gt;Carrie MacEwen: Okay, thank you.

00:45:49.269 --> 00:45:50.400
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much.
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: Thanks, Carrie.

00:45:50.400 --> 00:45:51.660
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Steve, the next one is you,
as well.

00:45:51.660 --> 00:45:57.050
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: Yes, so this is part of a similar
area of work, and with your permission, again,

00:45:57.050 --> 00:46:02.749
I’d like to invite Bruce Warner, who’s
Deputy Chief Pharmacist, to join me here at

00:46:02.749 --> 00:46:12.180
the Board table. So, during 2017 and ’18,
CCG guidance was published by NHS England

00:46:12.180 --> 00:46:17.119
and NHS clinical commissioners for firstly
items which should not be routinely prescribed

00:46:17.119 --> 00:46:22.990
in primary care; that was in November 2017,
a year ago, including recommendations on 18

00:46:22.990 --> 00:46:28.710
items. And then in March 2018, for conditions
for which over-the-counter items should not

00:46:28.710 --> 00:46:34.539
be routinely prescribed in primary care, so
that included recommendations on 35 minor

00:46:34.539 --> 00:46:41.319
conditions and vitamins, minerals and probiotics.
So, the clinical working group that has been

00:46:41.319 --> 00:46:47.950
underpinning and developing that work committed
to reviewing that guidance at least annually,

00:46:47.950 --> 00:46:53.940
identifying potential items to be retained,
removed or added to that guidance. And the

00:46:53.940 --> 00:46:57.819
first thing I would like to bring to the Board’s
attention today is that using established

00:46:57.819 --> 00:47:03.729
processes within that working group, a review
of the existing item recommendations from

00:47:03.729 --> 00:47:12.200
the November 2017 guidance has been undertaken.
And of those recommendations, we propose that

00:47:12.200 --> 00:47:17.690
guidance for one of the 18 items – that’s
rubefacients, including topical, non-steroidal,

00:47:17.690 --> 00:47:24.819
anti-inflammatory drugs – will be updated
to consider the exclusion of capsaicin cream,

00:47:24.819 --> 00:47:30.400
in line with NICE guidance. And –
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Steve, I didn’t hear that;

00:47:30.400 --> 00:47:36.369
can you say it again…?
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: It’s in your paper, Simon…

00:47:36.369 --> 00:47:40.170
And that recommendation will be part of the
consultation, which is a second part of what

00:47:40.170 --> 00:47:42.240
I’m going to bring to the attention of the
Board.

00:47:42.240 --> 00:47:46.440
So, secondly, the clinical working group proposed
that the following eight new items will be

00:47:46.440 --> 00:47:52.460
included in the draft CCG guidance for consultation.
So, these are, firstly, items of relatively

00:47:52.460 --> 00:47:58.339
low clinical effectiveness, or which are unsafe.
And so that list is amiodarone, which is a

00:47:58.339 --> 00:48:06.829
medication used to treat abnormal heart rhythms;
dronedarone, to treat chronic atrial fibrillation;

00:48:06.829 --> 00:48:12.789
bath and shower preparations for dry and pruritic
skin conditions; minocycline for acne; and

00:48:12.789 --> 00:48:17.900
silk garments for eczema and dermatitis. And
then three items which are clinically effective

00:48:17.900 --> 00:48:24.089
but where more cost-effective items are available,
so that’s aliskiren to treat blood pressure,

00:48:24.089 --> 00:48:29.349
blood pressure testing strips for type 2 diabetes,
and needles for pre-filled and reusable insulin

00:48:29.349 --> 00:48:34.319
pens. And I think it’s important with respect
to those latter two items for diabetes, what

00:48:34.319 --> 00:48:40.089
we are proposing in the consultation is not
that GPs don’t prescribe them, but they

00:48:40.089 --> 00:48:47.289
prescribe cost-effective products, i.e., products
that we know can be obtained at a lower price

00:48:47.289 --> 00:48:52.880
than the full range of items that we see at
the moment. So, this is not about removing

00:48:52.880 --> 00:48:57.290
them; it’s about cost-effective prescribing.
So, it should be noted that this is not a

00:48:57.290 --> 00:49:01.359
complete ban; it’s about selecting more
clinically or cost-effective alternative medicines

00:49:01.359 --> 00:49:07.700
and items, where appropriate. So, the Board,
in this part, are asked to approve a three-month

00:49:07.700 --> 00:49:12.869
formal public consultation on the draft CCG
guidance, and, subject to this, the draft

00:49:12.869 --> 00:49:16.709
guidance, and equality and health inequalities
impact assessment, will be published, and

00:49:16.709 --> 00:49:23.240
the consultation will run from today, the
28th of November, to the 28th of February

00:49:23.240 --> 00:49:26.390
2019.
And third and finally, I just want to say

00:49:26.390 --> 00:49:31.799
a few things on gluten-free food CCG guidance.
So, the Department of Health and Social Care

00:49:31.799 --> 00:49:35.890
has undertaken two national consultations
on the prescribing of gluten-free products

00:49:35.890 --> 00:49:43.859
in primary care, in March 2017 and August
2018. As a result of those consultations,

00:49:43.859 --> 00:49:47.930
regulations were laid in Parliament on the
6th of November, and subject to annulment

00:49:47.930 --> 00:49:55.999
by either House of Parliament, they will come
into force on the 4th of December 2018. Part

00:49:55.999 --> 00:49:59.890
15, borderline substances, and part 18A, schedule
1 of the drug tariff will be updated for the

00:49:59.890 --> 00:50:05.069
December edition, to reflect these changes.
So, in line with that, NHS England has developed

00:50:05.069 --> 00:50:10.630
guidance documents for – or a guidance document
for CCGs, setting out the national recommendations

00:50:10.630 --> 00:50:15.450
on the prescribing of gluten-free foods in
primary care. And this is intended to communicate

00:50:15.450 --> 00:50:20.729
the changes to the blacklisting of some GF
foods, and to support CCGs with the development

00:50:20.729 --> 00:50:26.059
of their local prescribing policies with respect
to gluten-free food. The guidance advises

00:50:26.059 --> 00:50:31.950
CCGs firstly to support prescribers to prescribe
in line with revised regulations, which allow

00:50:31.950 --> 00:50:36.269
for no gluten-free products to be prescribed
at NHS expense, other than gluten-free bread

00:50:36.269 --> 00:50:41.190
and/or gluten-free mixes, and secondly, that
patients in receipt of NHS prescriptions for

00:50:41.190 --> 00:50:45.809
gluten-free bread and/or mixes should be those
diagnosed by their doctors as suffering from

00:50:45.809 --> 00:50:51.430
established gluten-sensitive enteropathies,
including dermatitis, herpetiformis and coeliac

00:50:51.430 --> 00:50:56.089
disease. The guidance has been clinically
reviewed by the low-priority prescribing clinical

00:50:56.089 --> 00:51:01.700
working group, and I’m asking the Board
today to approve the publication of that CCG

00:51:01.700 --> 00:51:06.690
guidance and the equality and health inequalities
impact assessment.

00:51:06.690 --> 00:51:10.609
Anything that you would like to add, Bruce?
&gt;&gt;Bruce Warner: No, I think that covers it,

00:51:10.609 --> 00:51:14.489
I think, Steve. Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much. Any questions

00:51:14.489 --> 00:51:20.539
for Steve? I mean, I think the case is so
strong that it’s sort of – it doesn’t

00:51:20.539 --> 00:51:25.930
really need a lot of questions. Has anyone
got any – anything that anyone wants to

00:51:25.930 --> 00:51:29.060
raise? No?
Well, look, I think it’s another great bit

00:51:29.060 --> 00:51:34.049
of work. And I thank – and Bruce, I’m
sure you contributed a lot to this, as well,

00:51:34.049 --> 00:51:37.049
and sorry that you’ve been here and haven’t
been able to say very much.

00:51:37.049 --> 00:51:39.329
&gt;&gt;Bruce Warner: No, no, that’s fine.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: But I think it’s all been

00:51:39.329 --> 00:51:40.789
said, really.
&gt;&gt;Bruce Warner: We’ll – following the

00:51:40.789 --> 00:51:44.769
consultation, we’ll come back with a further
paper asking for approval. So, I’m sure

00:51:44.769 --> 00:51:47.160
we can have further discussion at that point.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah, great.

00:51:47.160 --> 00:51:50.509
&gt;&gt;Bruce Warner: Yes, both these programmes
of work are huge amounts of work, and so I

00:51:50.509 --> 00:51:54.309
would like to publicly thank the pharmacy
team under the leadership of Keith Ridge,

00:51:54.309 --> 00:51:59.829
Chief Pharmacist, in the development of the
guidance on prescribing, but also Ian’s

00:51:59.829 --> 00:52:04.309
team and my own team, particularly Annis and
Eiffer who are in the audience today, who’ve

00:52:04.309 --> 00:52:08.989
done a huge amount of work around the consultation
on the evidence-based interventions.

00:52:08.989 --> 00:52:13.039
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Very good. Well, thank you
very much, Bruce, and many thanks for coming.

00:52:13.039 --> 00:52:19.119
Right. Next item is item 7. Matthew?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Style: Thank you, Chair. I’d like

00:52:19.119 --> 00:52:26.840
to update the Board on progress with the negotiation
of the new voluntary medicines pricing scheme.

00:52:26.840 --> 00:52:29.950
Medicines pricing is governed by – there’s
something called the Pharmaceutical Price

00:52:29.950 --> 00:52:36.210
Regulations Scheme, which is a voluntary agreement
between government and the industry. The current

00:52:36.210 --> 00:52:42.410
voluntary scheme is due to expire on the 31st
of December, and the – hence an agreement

00:52:42.410 --> 00:52:46.849
for a new voluntary scheme has now been agreed
between the government, NHS England and the

00:52:46.849 --> 00:52:52.319
ABPI. Negotiations do continue on the final
details of the agreement, but good progress

00:52:52.319 --> 00:52:58.059
has been made, and the new scheme is intended
to run for five years through to the end of

00:52:58.059 --> 00:53:02.970
December 2024.
The voluntary scheme of course operates alongside

00:53:02.970 --> 00:53:09.600
a statutory scheme, which has recently been
refreshed and has made its way through the

00:53:09.600 --> 00:53:15.510
appropriate parliamentary procedures. In approaching
these negotiations at NHS England, we have

00:53:15.510 --> 00:53:20.440
been keen to strike a balance both between
ensuring that future spend on branded medicines

00:53:20.440 --> 00:53:27.549
is sustainable and affordable within our future
settlement, and – but also, that it is more

00:53:27.549 --> 00:53:33.459
predictable both for ourselves and for the
industry. But also, equally importantly, we’re

00:53:33.459 --> 00:53:38.869
very keen to ensure that the approach we take
to medicines pricing and the detail in the

00:53:38.869 --> 00:53:45.849
voluntary scheme supports faster uptake and
development of truly innovative treatments,

00:53:45.849 --> 00:53:51.359
and gets those transformative products to
patients as quickly as possible.

00:53:51.359 --> 00:53:58.109
The terms of the new scheme are such that
the allowable growth rate in branded medicines

00:53:58.109 --> 00:54:04.609
over the period will be 2% nominal in each
year of the scheme, such that any spend beyond

00:54:04.609 --> 00:54:10.579
those levels is repaid back to the NHS in
the form of a rebate. That rebate – well,

00:54:10.579 --> 00:54:17.490
clearly, as agreed with the government when
we did the long-term settlement negotiations

00:54:17.490 --> 00:54:21.140
in June, the proceeds of that rebate, as I
say, will flow back to the NHS to support

00:54:21.140 --> 00:54:25.799
the long-term plan and the priorities for
transformation and better patient care that

00:54:25.799 --> 00:54:34.839
will be embodied in that plan. The, as I say,
final – the final details of the deal will

00:54:34.839 --> 00:54:39.739
be signed in the coming weeks, such that the
new scheme can begin from the 1st of January.

00:54:39.739 --> 00:54:44.949
&gt;&gt;David Prior: I think this is a great deal
for the NHS, and I think it’s a good deal

00:54:44.949 --> 00:54:49.799
for industry, as well; it gives certainty.
I don’t know whether Simon or you – but

00:54:49.799 --> 00:54:55.490
if you can just comment on the differences
between the new PPRS scheme and the old one,

00:54:55.490 --> 00:54:59.099
which didn’t actually in the end quite deliver
what we wanted it to do.

00:54:59.099 --> 00:55:04.479
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: I think there’s a sense
that this is a significant improvement on

00:55:04.479 --> 00:55:10.359
what has gone before, both from the NHS point
of view and from – potentially from an industry

00:55:10.359 --> 00:55:17.180
point of view. We had a meeting of the new
Life Sciences Council last week, chaired by

00:55:17.180 --> 00:55:23.739
the Secretary of State for Business, and the
Secretary of State for Health, and the Chief

00:55:23.739 --> 00:55:27.760
Executive of AstraZeneca, with good industry
representation there, and I think there was

00:55:27.760 --> 00:55:33.709
a shared sense that at a time of some uncertainty
around Brexit, actually having this clarity

00:55:33.709 --> 00:55:37.799
as to what the next five years will look like
in terms of medicines is helpful to industry

00:55:37.799 --> 00:55:43.989
as well as to the NHS, and there are some
sort of win/win features around the promotion

00:55:43.989 --> 00:55:50.960
of uptake, which the headroom that the deal
creates will allow us to bring about. And

00:55:50.960 --> 00:56:00.150
as Matt said, crucially, from the NHS England
point of view, the fact that the net savings

00:56:00.150 --> 00:56:07.329
accrue back to the NHS on top of the 3.4%
funding settlement that we secured in June

00:56:07.329 --> 00:56:12.940
means that we can secure that funding to sustain
further innovation.

00:56:12.940 --> 00:56:18.279
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yes. I think congratulations
to all those on the – here and at the DH,

00:56:18.279 --> 00:56:25.069
who were involved in that. It’s a really
good outcome, yeah. Good, thanks very much,

00:56:25.069 --> 00:56:28.819
Matthew.
Adalimumab. Matthew?

00:56:28.819 --> 00:56:39.190
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yes, thank you, Chairman.
So, this is a good news story for the NHS,

00:56:39.190 --> 00:56:45.190
and I’ll start by thanking the team in – the
specialised commissioning team and some of

00:56:45.190 --> 00:56:50.059
Matt’s finance people, who’ve steered
this through over the last few months. This

00:56:50.059 --> 00:56:57.630
is adalimumab, which is the single highest-spend
drug in the NHS, which we currently spend

00:56:57.630 --> 00:57:03.949
£400&nbsp;million a year on, has come out of
patent, or come out of patent from 1st of

00:57:03.949 --> 00:57:13.339
December, and the team have worked across
the industry to run a complex – a sophisticated

00:57:13.339 --> 00:57:18.420
but quite rapid procurement process, with
the aim of doing two things. One was to create

00:57:18.420 --> 00:57:25.479
plurality in the market, as in, more than
one company supplying this medication, or

00:57:25.479 --> 00:57:31.749
the biosimilar equivalent of it. And the other,
to drive value for money for the NHS. The

00:57:31.749 --> 00:57:38.959
upshot of that is that we now have both the
original provider of the branded product,

00:57:38.959 --> 00:57:46.109
and four new suppliers of biosimilar equivalents,
are now supplying – will now be supplying

00:57:46.109 --> 00:57:52.781
to the NHS, saving the NHS £300&nbsp;million
through this negotiation. So, new contract

00:57:52.781 --> 00:58:01.131
value of £100&nbsp;million a year. We had estimated
that we could save £150&nbsp;million through

00:58:01.131 --> 00:58:06.140
– per year through this deal, so this is
done twice as well as that – through both

00:58:06.140 --> 00:58:13.780
the original creator of the drugs reducing
their price, as their patent comes to an end,

00:58:13.780 --> 00:58:20.900
and the pricing of the new vendors.
So, I think this is tremendous news for the

00:58:20.900 --> 00:58:28.890
NHS. This money is already planned to be recycled
into being spent on other things, creating

00:58:28.890 --> 00:58:35.029
more and better care for patients. And we
had forecast, when we looked at biological

00:58:35.029 --> 00:58:41.359
medicines and biosimilars a while ago in our
paper, we forecast that there were a potential

00:58:41.359 --> 00:58:48.529
£200&nbsp;million to £300&nbsp;million worth of
savings by the year 2021, so we have obviously

00:58:48.529 --> 00:58:52.969
exceeded that with the first deal that we’ve
done here, and there are a pipeline of other

00:58:52.969 --> 00:58:57.789
opportunities, not as large as this one, but
of other opportunities that – of leveraging

00:58:57.789 --> 00:59:02.730
this technology to improve care and value
for patients.

00:59:02.730 --> 00:59:06.390
&gt;&gt;David Prior: And the biologics are coming
in after the –

00:59:06.390 --> 00:59:10.979
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yeah, other drugs are
coming off patent in a variety of high-cost

00:59:10.979 --> 00:59:16.740
areas where we would expect to be able to
negotiate new pricing arrangements for the

00:59:16.740 --> 00:59:19.339
NHS as they come through, through our specialised
commissioning arrangements.

00:59:19.339 --> 00:59:24.400
&gt;&gt;David Prior: I think this drug is the highest-selling
drug ever; $18&nbsp;billion a year or something,

00:59:24.400 --> 00:59:28.380
across – around the world? I mean, it’s
been a phenomenal drug, but…

00:59:28.380 --> 00:59:31.559
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yeah.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Well, it’s a good news story.

00:59:31.559 --> 00:59:36.779
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: It’s a really good
news story, and it’s a good sign that in

00:59:36.779 --> 00:59:41.391
working in partnership with the industry,
we can make the NHS an attractive area for

00:59:41.391 --> 00:59:46.519
biosimilar companies to want to come and provide
products into whilst working with our existing

00:59:46.519 --> 00:59:49.920
partners to make sure that it still works
for them, as well.

00:59:49.920 --> 00:59:55.719
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Two very small point to add
to that, David. The first is that, as we said

00:59:55.719 --> 01:00:03.549
when this was announced, this is a demonstration
of the superior regulatory approach that the

01:00:03.549 --> 01:00:09.349
UK and Europe take to biosimilars than does
the United States, which enables us to have

01:00:09.349 --> 01:00:15.099
these savings faster. And secondly, there
is nothing automatic about the fact that,

01:00:15.099 --> 01:00:17.900
notwithstanding the patent expiry, we would
have got this level of savings. Actually,

01:00:17.900 --> 01:00:24.199
it’s a tribute to the way in which the procurement
was undertaken, and the various competitor

01:00:24.199 --> 01:00:28.949
pharma companies, including the originator,
responded to that. And I would be very surprised

01:00:28.949 --> 01:00:32.991
if we hadn’t got some of the best prices
in Europe on the back of it, notwithstanding

01:00:32.991 --> 01:00:37.839
the fact that the patent has expired everywhere.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: I think that’s beyond question,

01:00:37.839 --> 01:00:41.489
I think. It’ll be really – and I don’t
know whether it – how confidential it is.

01:00:41.489 --> 01:00:47.619
It may be more confidential, but as a case
study of how to price a biosimilar, it will

01:00:47.619 --> 01:01:00.640
be really interesting. Well, it’s terrific
anyway. So, any comments on that? No? I mean,

01:01:00.640 --> 01:01:07.559
it is just worth, just for the record, I mean,
this drug came out of Cambridge – out of

01:01:07.559 --> 01:01:15.230
the LMB in Cambridge 20 years ago or something,
and all that value that could have been captured

01:01:15.230 --> 01:01:19.719
for the UK economy, so much of it’s gone
to the US, and most of the manufacturing of

01:01:19.719 --> 01:01:23.440
these biologics is done in the US, and it
could have been done here.

01:01:23.440 --> 01:01:26.790
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: That’s the key lesson,
isn’t it?

01:01:26.790 --> 01:01:28.400
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Hm?
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: That’s the thing we

01:01:28.400 --> 01:01:32.180
need to learn from.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah, absolutely, yeah, we

01:01:32.180 --> 01:01:33.180
do. Yeah, Matthew?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Chair, I think as well

01:01:33.180 --> 01:01:36.289
as celebrating the human, we can support and
encourage the efforts of pharmacists and clinicians

01:01:36.289 --> 01:01:41.449
who are already doing fantastic work to prepare
for the switching that now must take place

01:01:41.449 --> 01:01:45.579
between the originator and the new products,
as well. That’s crucial to realising the

01:01:45.579 --> 01:01:49.079
savings here.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: And just one last point:

01:01:49.079 --> 01:01:56.319
I think – I remember these drugs first coming
into the NHS, and the tremendous impact that

01:01:56.319 --> 01:02:00.959
they had for patients, so I think we should
also bear in mind that this is a sign that

01:02:00.959 --> 01:02:07.740
we can bring the best drugs in the world to
patients in the NHS. The impact on patients

01:02:07.740 --> 01:02:13.640
with rheumatoid arthritis and other conditions
was spectacular at the time that it first

01:02:13.640 --> 01:02:18.029
came in. It was a transformational drug when
it first came through, and the ability to

01:02:18.029 --> 01:02:22.220
bring those through is dependent on our ability
to negotiate deals like this and therefore

01:02:22.220 --> 01:02:27.839
move onto the next generation of medications
that we want to bring through to patients.

01:02:27.839 --> 01:02:30.444
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah.
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: I would add, Chair, on

01:02:30.444 --> 01:02:35.920
your particular comment, I think there is
an opportunity for us to enter into some richer

01:02:35.920 --> 01:02:40.769
collaborations with industry that are about
getting a better deal for patients, as well

01:02:40.769 --> 01:02:44.640
as things that are good for the UK economy,
and this is I think one of the things that

01:02:44.640 --> 01:02:50.150
we’ll want to look at inevitably as part
of the long-term plan and what we do on supporting

01:02:50.150 --> 01:02:52.420
innovation in this country.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah, I mean, and how we can

01:02:52.420 --> 01:02:56.099
work with the pharma companies to drive down
the cost of developing these drugs and make

01:02:56.099 --> 01:02:58.359
them affordable, at the end of the day.
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Absolutely.

01:02:58.359 --> 01:03:03.189
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah. Okay, now – well, Matthew,
thank you very much for that. Jane, empowering

01:03:03.189 --> 01:03:07.150
people and communities.
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: Thank you, Chair. I’ll

01:03:07.150 --> 01:03:13.209
invite Neil Churchill up, who is our responsible
director for experience, participation and

01:03:13.209 --> 01:03:19.940
equalities. So, this is the third report of
the Empowering People and Communities Taskforce.

01:03:19.940 --> 01:03:27.049
As you know – or the Board will know – we
set this up for a year. So, the final meeting

01:03:27.049 --> 01:03:35.269
is due next month, in December. And this particular
paper sets out an update, really, on what

01:03:35.269 --> 01:03:39.799
we’ve done on the priority areas around
personalised care and frailty, and attached

01:03:39.799 --> 01:03:45.029
to the paper are letters that have been written
by the two co-chairs to the SROs for those.

01:03:45.029 --> 01:03:50.249
We also have updated on the work we’ve done
to support engagement with the long-term plan,

01:03:50.249 --> 01:03:54.579
and I think that’s been really, really important,
and actually, we’ve done more this time

01:03:54.579 --> 01:03:59.449
than we’ve ever done before. It also has
helped us to determine the future for the

01:03:59.449 --> 01:04:06.700
NHS Assembly. And it also sets out principles
on how we want to engage more with the voluntary

01:04:06.700 --> 01:04:10.880
sector, community and social enterprise sector
going forward, which I think is really important.

01:04:10.880 --> 01:04:16.000
As the Board will know, we’ve previously
reported on the work we’ve done with the

01:04:16.000 --> 01:04:19.760
other priority areas like cancer and mental
health, and again, it gives an update on some

01:04:19.760 --> 01:04:25.760
of the changes that have happened since then.
In December – so there’ll be one final

01:04:25.760 --> 01:04:31.970
report in early 2019, and the plan is that
we also include some – an update on digital

01:04:31.970 --> 01:04:37.589
transformation at that one.
And before I hand over to Victor as one of

01:04:37.589 --> 01:04:42.140
the co-chairs, I just wanted to make the point
that I think the taskforce has been really

01:04:42.140 --> 01:04:48.170
instrumental in helping us think through how
we engage in a better way. It has really prioritised

01:04:48.170 --> 01:04:53.200
or looked at the priorities. It’s looked
at how we can improve some of the health inequalities

01:04:53.200 --> 01:04:59.349
that we see across the system, and it’s
looked at a very different way of engaging,

01:04:59.349 --> 01:05:05.230
so both using social media, having things
like Twitter chats, but also, actually having

01:05:05.230 --> 01:05:12.130
sort of deep-dive workshops with relevant
people, members of the public, around what

01:05:12.130 --> 01:05:16.950
it means to them. And I think that’s brought
a very different approach to the way we’ve

01:05:16.950 --> 01:05:21.289
done this before.
And I’d just like to say a thank you both

01:05:21.289 --> 01:05:25.299
to Neil and his team, but also, to Victor
and Michelle, for the work they’ve led.

01:05:25.299 --> 01:05:30.260
Their passion and commitment to actually making
sure this works has been phenomenal, and has

01:05:30.260 --> 01:05:34.009
made a real difference to how we’ve been
able to engage. And I think the work of the

01:05:34.009 --> 01:05:38.880
taskforce and the way we’ve been able to
report this over the last few months has really

01:05:38.880 --> 01:05:42.769
shown a difference. So, I will commend the
report to you. I will hand over to Victor

01:05:42.769 --> 01:05:46.499
as one of the co-chairs to see if he wants
to add anything, and Neil’s obviously here

01:05:46.499 --> 01:05:50.489
as the lead director who will also be able
to help with any questions that people may

01:05:50.489 --> 01:05:52.049
ask.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you very much, Jane.

01:05:52.049 --> 01:05:55.200
Neil, shall we come – shall we just go to
Victor first then come to you, is that all

01:05:55.200 --> 01:05:58.739
right? Victor?
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: So, the first thing I’d

01:05:58.739 --> 01:06:03.099
like to do is thank all the people that have
been involved with us in this. It’s – the

01:06:03.099 --> 01:06:08.319
approach has been different. It’s different
to the way we – NHS England or NHS has done

01:06:08.319 --> 01:06:13.650
things in that we designed a process that
was inclusive of both patients and the public,

01:06:13.650 --> 01:06:19.509
and then we ran the process live, as it were.
And we’ve had – you know, in each area

01:06:19.509 --> 01:06:22.759
that we’ve looked at, we’ve had hundreds
and indeed, in some cases, thousands of people

01:06:22.759 --> 01:06:28.349
sending in – using social media and adding
their voice to this. And that’s produced

01:06:28.349 --> 01:06:35.239
some insights into the experience of the interventions
that we’re trying to – the experience

01:06:35.239 --> 01:06:40.640
and outcomes of some of the interventions
that we’re trying to run. So, for example,

01:06:40.640 --> 01:06:48.219
the work we did on cancer, I thought brought
out some really new, I think, insights into

01:06:48.219 --> 01:06:54.290
the impact on the cancer programme on BME
groups, and on those groups that are – and

01:06:54.290 --> 01:06:59.920
young people, and brought out issues of inequality,
and the same goes for mental health, where

01:06:59.920 --> 01:07:05.961
some work was done on how we co-produce mental
health programmes, and then recently, work

01:07:05.961 --> 01:07:12.609
done on frailty, where the issues of definition
and experience, but also the notion that frailty

01:07:12.609 --> 01:07:16.880
isn’t just something that affects elderly
people – it is a young person’s issue,

01:07:16.880 --> 01:07:23.099
as well – actually kind of changes the way
in which we look at programme design and allocation

01:07:23.099 --> 01:07:26.410
of resource.
What I’d like to do is drawn attention to

01:07:26.410 --> 01:07:31.609
the work that I’d like – hopefully like
the Board to approve, which is the work on

01:07:31.609 --> 01:07:37.359
the VCSE principles. Now, the reason why this
work was being done is that there’s a view

01:07:37.359 --> 01:07:43.410
and an experience that the current arrangements
are neither clear nor necessarily helpful,

01:07:43.410 --> 01:07:48.769
on both sides, actually, to understanding
how we build a strong partnership with the

01:07:48.769 --> 01:07:56.299
VCSE sector. And therefore, what we have done
is work with that sector and with NHS England

01:07:56.299 --> 01:08:02.859
on how a set of principles might be brought
together to enable better engagement, clearer

01:08:02.859 --> 01:08:08.049
engagement, more transparent engagement, and
bring in the seldom heard voices that I think

01:08:08.049 --> 01:08:11.589
will add value to programmes going forward,
and in particular, in relation to the long-term

01:08:11.589 --> 01:08:15.970
plan. So, I’d like to invite the Board to
approve that – those principles, and then

01:08:15.970 --> 01:08:21.920
the next step will be to roll them out and
ensure that they are practised.

01:08:21.920 --> 01:08:31.279
In conclusion, the taskforce I think has hopefully
shown that actually engaging the public is

01:08:31.279 --> 01:08:37.029
a safe and appropriate thing to do, and helpful,
and actually, some of the fears around doing

01:08:37.029 --> 01:08:41.420
that have turned out to be chimeras; they’re
not real, and the public can add value to

01:08:41.420 --> 01:08:47.809
what we do if we design it properly. And that
insight may well be helpful to the design

01:08:47.809 --> 01:08:54.259
of the NHS Assembly, which I hope that is
the case. And also sets a high standard for

01:08:54.259 --> 01:08:59.620
this work going forward. I think that the
NHS is something which was created by the

01:08:59.620 --> 01:09:03.839
public, and therefore, should engage and involve
the public if you wish it to both save money

01:09:03.839 --> 01:09:08.660
and increase its efficacy. So, this is a small
step in that direction that hopefully will

01:09:08.660 --> 01:09:13.920
act as a catalyst, in particular, the areas
of inequality, which you know are close to

01:09:13.920 --> 01:09:21.460
my heart, we’ve driven through this process,
and I think shown the – that an insightful

01:09:21.460 --> 01:09:25.290
view and partnership with people who are at
the sharp end of the inverse care law can

01:09:25.290 --> 01:09:32.210
help us both save money and increase programme
effectiveness. We have the last meeting, which

01:09:32.210 --> 01:09:37.540
will look at the digital in the – using
exactly the same methodology as we’ve done

01:09:37.540 --> 01:09:42.480
for other programmes. Going forward, the last
meeting – or the last report, which I won’t

01:09:42.480 --> 01:09:49.410
be here for, will be looking at the improvement
action to support engagement, because I think

01:09:49.410 --> 01:09:55.610
it is an ongoing story how we do that, developing
closer partnerships with the voluntary sector,

01:09:55.610 --> 01:10:00.500
assuming that that – the principles are
approved here. And supporting better empowerment

01:10:00.500 --> 01:10:08.739
of people within the NHS priority programmes
that will come out of the Forward View. So,

01:10:08.739 --> 01:10:13.820
hopefully, the Board and colleagues have found
this work useful over the year, and I hope

01:10:13.820 --> 01:10:16.369
the learning will continue into other programmes.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Neil, did you want to comment?

01:10:16.369 --> 01:10:24.329
&gt;&gt;Neil Churchill: I think just to pay tribute
to Victor and his leadership on this agenda.

01:10:24.329 --> 01:10:27.620
I think – I had an email from one of the
voluntary-sector leaders who’s a member

01:10:27.620 --> 01:10:33.909
of the taskforce, who commented how positive
he’d found it, to witness these conversations

01:10:33.909 --> 01:10:41.270
between leaders in the system and citizens
who are marginalised or ignored in a lot of

01:10:41.270 --> 01:10:46.260
conversations, and to see that those conversations
result in listening and action. And he said,

01:10:46.260 --> 01:10:50.619
‘Can we have more like this, please?’
So, I think it was designed to strengthen

01:10:50.619 --> 01:10:55.219
us in the areas of patient and public engagement,
and patient experience. I think Victor’s

01:10:55.219 --> 01:11:00.620
really led that from the front with a real
moral authority, and we’ve definitely heard

01:11:00.620 --> 01:11:04.550
things that we wouldn’t have heard otherwise.
So, just say thank you, Victor, and I think

01:11:04.550 --> 01:11:09.690
we do want to build on this and take it on.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Noel?

01:11:09.690 --> 01:11:18.989
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: Just to focus on the VCSE dimension
of the paper, which has come an awfully long

01:11:18.989 --> 01:11:26.969
way since we first started talking about co-production
two years. You mentioned, Victor, in the paper

01:11:26.969 --> 01:11:34.449
the idea that link workers should be an integral
part of our delivery model in areas like integrated

01:11:34.449 --> 01:11:41.449
care and prevention and wellbeing. Could you
just sort of articulate your conclusion about

01:11:41.449 --> 01:11:46.559
that, in terms of the long-term plan? Do you
think link workers should be an integral piece

01:11:46.559 --> 01:11:51.750
of the way that we design the next wave of
care model in the community?

01:11:51.750 --> 01:11:59.179
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Well, the short answer
is yes. I think we should be – the lesson

01:11:59.179 --> 01:12:03.429
of – well, there’s two things. First of
all, the lesson of the work on the taskforce

01:12:03.429 --> 01:12:07.380
is that we can engage the public and receive
insights that are powerful and helpful to

01:12:07.380 --> 01:12:13.530
– and leaders of the system can talk to
the public in a way that actually… Secondly,

01:12:13.530 --> 01:12:20.920
the VCSE sector is – should be adding value
in a more organised way, and the notion of

01:12:20.920 --> 01:12:24.990
link workers is a suggestion as to how that
might work. I’ll invite Neil to say more

01:12:24.990 --> 01:12:30.050
about the detail, but I think we need a more
structured approach in our relationship with

01:12:30.050 --> 01:12:34.350
the VCSE sector if it’s going to add value,
and I can’t think of an area in the Ten

01:12:34.350 --> 01:12:38.380
Year Forward View where that isn’t the case.
So – yeah, I think that’s where we got

01:12:38.380 --> 01:12:44.110
to. I don’t know whether you want to say
more about…

01:12:44.110 --> 01:12:52.349
&gt;&gt;Neil Churchill: Well, I was just going to
focus on – like I say, that was very much

01:12:52.349 --> 01:12:56.250
part of the conversation we had with James
Sanderson and his comprehensive model in relation

01:12:56.250 --> 01:13:02.909
to personalised care, and in particular, how
those workers would link in with voluntary

01:13:02.909 --> 01:13:08.449
organisations who are in touch with more marginalised
and excluded groups of the population. So,

01:13:08.449 --> 01:13:12.010
we had a good conversation about that, and
I think that we’ll see how that develops

01:13:12.010 --> 01:13:15.500
in the thinking around the plan.
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: I mean, I think to be

01:13:15.500 --> 01:13:24.489
honest, I think that the impact of work by
VCSE groups with those people that are at

01:13:24.489 --> 01:13:28.130
the sharp end of the inverse care law isn’t
just about link workers; it’s about how

01:13:28.130 --> 01:13:34.640
we design public health in those places, such
that we can actually manage demand better.

01:13:34.640 --> 01:13:40.110
And example I keep giving is one from my own
organisation, where I know that in two or

01:13:40.110 --> 01:13:46.719
three areas, my clients are going to be the
ones turning up at A&amp;E in winter, largely

01:13:46.719 --> 01:13:51.840
because we’ve yet to actually engage fully
with the system in order to assist in the

01:13:51.840 --> 01:13:56.900
management of demand.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: So, just adding to what Neil

01:13:56.900 --> 01:14:03.000
said, to thank Victor for his leadership on
this, but I think it’s not just a process.

01:14:03.000 --> 01:14:07.010
I think there are some – to state the obvious,
some very important substantive questions

01:14:07.010 --> 01:14:10.440
which have arisen from this, and I just wanted
to give one example which is referenced in

01:14:10.440 --> 01:14:15.059
the – in appendix two, the letter of the
23rd of October, which has now played into

01:14:15.059 --> 01:14:20.530
one of the big debates we’re still having
in the long-term plan, actually, around frailty.

01:14:20.530 --> 01:14:27.730
And what this work kind of really has triggered
a big conversation about is, frailty obviously

01:14:27.730 --> 01:14:34.239
through a lens of supporting older people
is a big move towards – is identified in

01:14:34.239 --> 01:14:40.690
care-of-the-elderly medicine, previously known
as geriatrics, as a kind of thing, a concept

01:14:40.690 --> 01:14:45.920
in its own right. But what this has I think
pointed out, is actually, there are many people

01:14:45.920 --> 01:14:52.159
who are frail who aren’t elderly. Secondly,
actually labelling people frail is a very

01:14:52.159 --> 01:14:58.320
disempowering term that actually, people don’t
like. And thirdly, we should probably be conceptualising

01:14:58.320 --> 01:15:04.670
this as high-need, of whom, in some areas,
many will be frail, not all of whom will be

01:15:04.670 --> 01:15:09.250
elderly, rather than using frailty per se
as the way of encapsulating a new, more activist

01:15:09.250 --> 01:15:14.360
community health model. So, really, it was
that – it was your sort of challenge which

01:15:14.360 --> 01:15:19.290
has triggered all of that debate amongst the
many partners in forming the long-term plan,

01:15:19.290 --> 01:15:24.690
and which we need to conclude on very shortly.
So, thank you for that, as well.

01:15:24.690 --> 01:15:29.809
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: Can I just very briefly – I
think this also relates to Carrie’s comment

01:15:29.809 --> 01:15:35.909
earlier around demedicalising care. I think
it’s really striking how, when you look

01:15:35.909 --> 01:15:41.219
at how we connect the NHS to wider societal
support, there’s actually a huge enthusiasm

01:15:41.219 --> 01:15:45.930
that’s being led by the profession, actually.
So, the biggest advocates of the kind of social

01:15:45.930 --> 01:15:49.840
prescribing or referral models, at the moment,
are at the Royal College of GPs.

01:15:49.840 --> 01:15:55.079
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Jane?
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: Just – I think just picking

01:15:55.079 --> 01:16:01.110
up Noel’s point, as well, there is something
really powerful about not only the work that

01:16:01.110 --> 01:16:06.639
we’ve done nationally, but also, what happens
at a local level. And actually having people

01:16:06.639 --> 01:16:11.160
that work in the voluntary sector that actually
look after each other and support communities

01:16:11.160 --> 01:16:15.570
is really important, and you and I were talking
earlier, we’ve got Bren in the audience

01:16:15.570 --> 01:16:20.400
who actually leads this in Gloucestershire,
in a significant way. That’s one example

01:16:20.400 --> 01:16:26.000
where having communities supporting each other
and actually looking after the most vulnerable,

01:16:26.000 --> 01:16:30.330
and enabling them to live their lives and
get the best outcomes, is a really important

01:16:30.330 --> 01:16:34.090
thing. So, we look at it nationally, do the
work we’ve done nationally, led by Victor

01:16:34.090 --> 01:16:37.559
and Michelle in the taskforce, but also, we
need to remember that actually, to make the

01:16:37.559 --> 01:16:41.460
difference on the ground, we need to have
– we need to link into those that deliver

01:16:41.460 --> 01:16:46.260
it day in, day out. And the use of the voluntary
sector and social prescribing, as Ian’s

01:16:46.260 --> 01:16:50.250
just pointed out, I think is a really powerful
way of doing that, and has really significant

01:16:50.250 --> 01:16:54.500
outcomes.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Excellent. Well, thank you

01:16:54.500 --> 01:16:58.929
very much. Just to echo what Jane’s said,
because Bren is in the audience over there.

01:16:58.929 --> 01:17:03.630
We had a day in Parliament two week ago, just
seeing what you – what people can do in

01:17:03.630 --> 01:17:10.010
their local communities. It is very powerful,
and very inspirational, actually.

01:17:10.010 --> 01:17:19.000
Right. Moving on to something less inspirational,
sort of finance and performance. Matthew?

01:17:19.000 --> 01:17:28.810
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: Make it inspirational…
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Thank you. Yes, I’d

01:17:28.810 --> 01:17:34.400
like to start by drawing the Board’s attention.
As you read through the paper, which gives

01:17:34.400 --> 01:17:38.930
you kind of all the breakdown of performance
over the last month, and a comparison to the

01:17:38.930 --> 01:17:43.330
last year, what I’d like to just pull out
is the theme that’s coming through here

01:17:43.330 --> 01:17:48.980
is the extent to which services are being
redesigned, the pathways are being redesigned

01:17:48.980 --> 01:17:54.639
and the patient experience is changing. So,
I just thought I’d join some of those up

01:17:54.639 --> 01:18:00.630
for you.
In elective care, we’ve seen GP referrals

01:18:00.630 --> 01:18:09.510
have risen this year by 0.2%. Last year, they
actually fell compared to the year before.

01:18:09.510 --> 01:18:12.761
If you were to go back two or three years,
you’d find that the GP referrals were going

01:18:12.761 --> 01:18:20.840
up 4% or 5% every year. That doesn’t happen
by magic; that’s happened because of a series

01:18:20.840 --> 01:18:25.500
of innovations in primary care, to be able
to manage patients closer to home, closer

01:18:25.500 --> 01:18:29.650
to primary care, and not require everyone
to go into outpatients. So, for example, for

01:18:29.650 --> 01:18:38.969
musculoskeletal services, we’ve now – in
178 CCGs, we now have MSK triage services.

01:18:38.969 --> 01:18:45.070
In the CCGs that have put those in place,
we have seen a 10% reduction in referrals

01:18:45.070 --> 01:18:53.449
into orthopaedic outpatients. That ties in
with, we now have 12 specialty handbooks distributed

01:18:53.449 --> 01:18:59.010
addressing how to redesign care around particular
specialties, and we’ve got first-contact

01:18:59.010 --> 01:19:04.430
practitioners across primary care now managing
patients close to their home and relieving

01:19:04.430 --> 01:19:11.199
the burden of hospital.
In the emergency care setting, you’ll see

01:19:11.199 --> 01:19:19.370
in the numbers a 4.3% overall increase in
A&amp;E attendances. When you look below the surface

01:19:19.370 --> 01:19:25.690
on that, that’s a less than 2% growth in
what we call type 1 A&amp;E, that – actual hospital

01:19:25.690 --> 01:19:31.480
A&amp;Es, what the public would consider to be
a hospital A&amp;E. And an 11% growth in the use

01:19:31.480 --> 01:19:36.440
of urgent treatment centres, minor injuries
units and walk-in centres, which have been

01:19:36.440 --> 01:19:42.210
a specific focus of our development over the
last couple of years, to create those alternate

01:19:42.210 --> 01:19:47.929
pathways to care, keeping people away from
A&amp;E so that A&amp;Es can focus on patients who

01:19:47.929 --> 01:19:57.849
actually need to be there. 111, as our telephone-based
service, has – saw – took 1.3&nbsp;million

01:19:57.849 --> 01:20:04.119
calls last month; that’s a nearly 6% increase
on the same month last year, of which over

01:20:04.119 --> 01:20:08.440
half of them got to speak to a clinician – the
patients got to speak to a clinician. That’s

01:20:08.440 --> 01:20:14.350
a 10% increase on the position last year.
So, increasingly, 111 is able to solve people’s

01:20:14.350 --> 01:20:20.139
problems, and not just direct them to another
service. And we now have an online app-based

01:20:20.139 --> 01:20:24.989
version of that available across the country,
and in two thirds of the country, that’s

01:20:24.989 --> 01:20:30.860
full integrated into the 111 services we will
get to the whole of the country.

01:20:30.860 --> 01:20:38.510
Our digital services: we’ve seen 2.5&nbsp;million
more visits to the NHS website this year compared

01:20:38.510 --> 01:20:43.960
to the same period last year. We’ve had
620,000 visits to the app library, where we

01:20:43.960 --> 01:20:49.320
have 75 apps, and another 120 going through
approval processes to help people manage their

01:20:49.320 --> 01:20:56.120
own care. And we have – what will become
a ubiquitous NHS app is now in testing with

01:20:56.120 --> 01:21:01.440
30 GP practices to roll out across the country.
And then when we look at people in hospital,

01:21:01.440 --> 01:21:09.500
we’ve seen a reduction in delayed transfers
of care this year of 792 beds – that’s

01:21:09.500 --> 01:21:16.270
a 14% reduction – and a reduction in the
number of patients in hospital for over 21

01:21:16.270 --> 01:21:23.841
days of 13%. That’s releasing nearly 2,500
beds back into the hospital sector to care

01:21:23.841 --> 01:21:29.500
for people, and the total number of occupied
bed days has fallen by 1.5%. So, there’s

01:21:29.500 --> 01:21:34.610
a whole raft now going on across the whole
of the country in all sectors, which are saying,

01:21:34.610 --> 01:21:38.790
you don’t have to suck everybody into hospital;
there are ways of caring for people using

01:21:38.790 --> 01:21:43.139
digital technology, using the telephone, using
primary care in different ways, to keep people

01:21:43.139 --> 01:21:48.329
away. And I think that’s – when we see
the long-term plan come out, we will see that

01:21:48.329 --> 01:21:53.350
story will be writ through it about, how do
we become preventative, how do we become closer

01:21:53.350 --> 01:21:57.909
to communities, how do we keep people away
from becoming an inpatient admission?

01:21:57.909 --> 01:22:05.619
In terms of performance, A&amp;E performance in
the last month was at 89.1%; that’s up from

01:22:05.619 --> 01:22:12.260
88.9% in the previous month. We’ve seen
ambulance performance continue to improve

01:22:12.260 --> 01:22:23.800
against the new standards. Elective waiting
list: the compliance with the 18-week standard

01:22:23.800 --> 01:22:37.039
fell from 87.2% in August to 86.7% in September.
There’s a big focus at the moment on long-waiters.

01:22:37.039 --> 01:22:41.040
We set a target of halving the number of people
who waited over 52 weeks; that’s going in

01:22:41.040 --> 01:22:45.619
the wrong direction at the moment. So, there
is very close focus; hospital by hospital,

01:22:45.619 --> 01:22:51.840
we are still focused on delivering that halving
of the number of long-waiters before the end

01:22:51.840 --> 01:22:57.969
of the year.
In primary care, we’ve brought forward the

01:22:57.969 --> 01:23:03.239
improving access, extended access for primary
care objective to this side of Christmas in

01:23:03.239 --> 01:23:08.500
order to support this winter, and we’re
currently at 98% of the country; 98% of the

01:23:08.500 --> 01:23:19.300
population has access to extended hours primary
care. GP trainees: we set a target of an extra

01:23:19.300 --> 01:23:25.001
3,250 trainees. We’ve actually managed to
recruit 3,473, so that’s a 10% increase

01:23:25.001 --> 01:23:31.539
on last year. We are – we’ve virtually
hit the increase of 5,000 in primary care

01:23:31.539 --> 01:23:38.400
staff growth that we were aiming for, and
we are on track to invest £800&nbsp;million more

01:23:38.400 --> 01:23:44.489
in primary care technology and estate over
five years. We are still struggling with our

01:23:44.489 --> 01:23:50.530
ambition to add 5,000 GPs, and there’s a
considerable work going on around improving

01:23:50.530 --> 01:23:56.830
GP retention, reducing the workload for GPs,
and encouraging people to come back into the

01:23:56.830 --> 01:24:03.989
workforce who stepped out of it, as well as
our international recruitment. And in mental

01:24:03.989 --> 01:24:09.789
health, we are on track to ensure that 100%
of CCGs meet the mental health investment

01:24:09.789 --> 01:24:17.050
standard this year: 90% last year, 85% the
year before. And in cancer, we are struggling

01:24:17.050 --> 01:24:24.579
around the 62-day target. We have seen a 13.6%
increase in demand this year. There’s been

01:24:24.579 --> 01:24:31.400
a 10.7% increase in activity, in cancer activity.
So, big increase in demand, big increase in

01:24:31.400 --> 01:24:36.719
activity, but the consequence of that is that
we are moving away from the 85% cancer target

01:24:36.719 --> 01:24:43.690
and the 62-day target, not towards it. We’ve
invested another £10&nbsp;million in increasing

01:24:43.690 --> 01:24:48.840
capacity in cancer across the country, in
order to try and get that back under control.

01:24:48.840 --> 01:24:56.550
It is an anomaly in the way in which we count
the waiting times, that as we deal with patients

01:24:56.550 --> 01:25:01.409
who’ve waited too long, the performance
figures will get worse before they get better,

01:25:01.409 --> 01:25:07.260
but we are – there’s very close engagement
now. In fact, the reason I will be presenting

01:25:07.260 --> 01:25:15.190
the winter target report shortly, and not
Neil Permain, is that he is out as part of

01:25:15.190 --> 01:25:19.460
a team going and visiting every cancer centre
to talk through what they’re doing in order

01:25:19.460 --> 01:25:25.780
to recover the cancer targets. So, considerable
work going on, and I will, with your permission

01:25:25.780 --> 01:25:28.050
– I’ll stop for any questions, and then
I’ll move on to talk about specifically

01:25:28.050 --> 01:25:30.079
what we’re doing on winter.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Just one question.

01:25:30.079 --> 01:25:34.679
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Please?
&gt;&gt;David Prior: On cancer –

01:25:34.679 --> 01:25:36.990
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yes.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: – could you just give us

01:25:36.990 --> 01:25:42.560
a bit more – sort of slightly more granular
on the sort of cancer side? Because I think

01:25:42.560 --> 01:25:46.050
it’s mainly – a lot of the issue and the
extra demand is around prostate, I think.

01:25:46.050 --> 01:25:52.280
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yes.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Just give us a bit of a view

01:25:52.280 --> 01:25:57.070
on that, could you?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Do you… But – so…

01:25:57.070 --> 01:26:00.210
Thank you, a bit of an agreement is helpful.
The – it’s over a number of cancers, but

01:26:00.210 --> 01:26:10.510
the most dramatic increase has been around
prostate cancer, or around urological referrals.

01:26:10.510 --> 01:26:15.270
That is good news to the extent that – it’s
– the bad news is, it’s taken us by surprise

01:26:15.270 --> 01:26:18.880
that it should increase so much, so quickly.
So, we’re stumbling a bit to respond to

01:26:18.880 --> 01:26:25.349
demand, but we are – we need to identify
cancers earlier, which means people need to

01:26:25.349 --> 01:26:28.860
come forward with their symptoms earlier in
order that we can get them through the diagnostic

01:26:28.860 --> 01:26:35.420
process and identify – and there’s been
a very large spike in people coming forward

01:26:35.420 --> 01:26:43.260
with urological concerns, what they call ‘query
cancer’, concerns that they might have cancer.

01:26:43.260 --> 01:26:50.760
Interestingly, the numbers of cancers that
we are identifying in the increase are proportionally

01:26:50.760 --> 01:26:54.250
not wholly dissimilar to the numbers that
we were seeing in the previous cohort. So,

01:26:54.250 --> 01:26:59.460
these are not wasted appointments; we are
still finding cancers that wouldn’t have

01:26:59.460 --> 01:27:04.320
been diagnosed if those patients hadn’t
come forward. So, we – whilst I talk about

01:27:04.320 --> 01:27:08.260
demand management in other areas and making
sure the right patients come forward, I don’t

01:27:08.260 --> 01:27:11.969
talk about demand management in cancer; we
need to identify more, get them treated more

01:27:11.969 --> 01:27:16.199
quickly, in order to improve our outcomes.
That means the challenge therefore is on us

01:27:16.199 --> 01:27:20.381
to be able to deal with the capacity; as the
public get the message, we need to be able

01:27:20.381 --> 01:27:29.250
to step up and respond into the – a nearly
14% growth in demand, with a nearly 11% growth

01:27:29.250 --> 01:27:36.219
in capacity, is a pretty impressive response
by the NHS, but it’s not as big an increase

01:27:36.219 --> 01:27:43.099
in capacity as the demand increase has been.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Just on that point, Steve,

01:27:43.099 --> 01:27:52.139
is the 62-day waiting time target as appropriate
for prostate cancer as it is for other cancers?

01:27:52.139 --> 01:27:59.969
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: So, the prostate pathway is
a pathway in which there is a fair amount

01:27:59.969 --> 01:28:08.020
of treatment choice, including, in some patients,
a watchful waiting-type approach rather than

01:28:08.020 --> 01:28:13.610
an intervention approach. So, it is true that
the feedback that we’ve had from clinicians

01:28:13.610 --> 01:28:22.599
is that if we were to examine one of the pathways
to test whether the 62-day pathway works well

01:28:22.599 --> 01:28:27.119
in individual pathways, prostate would be
the one to test. And the cancer team are,

01:28:27.119 --> 01:28:33.550
indeed, at the moment, with their clinical
advisors, looking at that – at exactly that

01:28:33.550 --> 01:28:44.510
question, to look to see whether the prostate
pathway within the 62-day target is set as

01:28:44.510 --> 01:28:49.469
appropriately as it should be to take into
account the clinical nuances in that pathway.

01:28:49.469 --> 01:28:55.289
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: I think the thing we
have to ask is, is the mix of cancers changing?

01:28:55.289 --> 01:28:59.350
Because the reason we have an 85% target is
to reflect that some we should be doing better

01:28:59.350 --> 01:29:06.000
on. So, dermatology and breast performance
is around 90%, so the idea – the clinical

01:29:06.000 --> 01:29:11.909
appropriateness with some would be much quicker,
some would be a lower performance. So, 85%

01:29:11.909 --> 01:29:16.889
reflects that, but if the number of prostate
patients goes up disproportionately to the

01:29:16.889 --> 01:29:21.659
others, then the average that was calculated
previously becomes inappropriate, and there

01:29:21.659 --> 01:29:25.950
is a risk that patients are being pushed into
receiving treatment which, actually, patient

01:29:25.950 --> 01:29:30.619
choice would be watchful waiting. So, we need
to be really smart about that.

01:29:30.619 --> 01:29:37.250
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: So, the direction will undoubtedly
be, as Matthew has said, towards maximising

01:29:37.250 --> 01:29:43.560
early diagnosis. So, the direction of travel
within cancer will be to diagnose as quickly

01:29:43.560 --> 01:29:51.309
as possible, and then after every diagnosis,
as Carrie actually highlighted in the discussion

01:29:51.309 --> 01:29:57.030
we had on evidence-based interventions – and
it is as true in cancer as it is in any other

01:29:57.030 --> 01:30:02.140
condition, and prostate is a very good example
of this – to then have a shared decision-type

01:30:02.140 --> 01:30:07.190
discussion with patients as to sort of most
appropriate treatment. Clearly, it’s also

01:30:07.190 --> 01:30:14.349
important that treatment is then done in a
reasonable time, but actually, what we increasingly

01:30:14.349 --> 01:30:22.619
want, for reasons that we have described,
is to maximise – is to focus on getting

01:30:22.619 --> 01:30:28.170
a diagnose as early as possible, and that
is both around encouraging patients to come

01:30:28.170 --> 01:30:32.199
forward, which is the story behind prostate,
which we discussed at the last Board around

01:30:32.199 --> 01:30:37.030
the publicity around prostate cancer, but
also, then to have our pathways to be able

01:30:37.030 --> 01:30:42.219
to make the diagnosis as quickly as possible.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Any questions? Noel?

01:30:42.219 --> 01:30:49.220
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: I just wanted to echo a theme
in this paper which Matthew alluded to. Because

01:30:49.220 --> 01:30:56.139
I think it’s important, and summarised well,
which is how we’re beginning to see the

01:30:56.139 --> 01:31:02.320
waterfall of digital offers coming through.
Now, the reason why that’s important is

01:31:02.320 --> 01:31:07.239
because we spent the last year building – last
two years building the tracks on which these

01:31:07.239 --> 01:31:13.170
trains run, to some of the highest levels
of resilience and some of the most important

01:31:13.170 --> 01:31:18.719
national infrastructure for the NHS, and now
the trains are being delivered. What’s pleasing

01:31:18.719 --> 01:31:24.909
about it is, one, the take-up rates are really
positive. The adoption rates in different

01:31:24.909 --> 01:31:30.870
areas of clinical and patient practice are
also very positive. And I think patients will

01:31:30.870 --> 01:31:37.619
begin to see that what we have created in
the back office for the last two years, now

01:31:37.619 --> 01:31:43.619
allows us to put some compelling digital offers
into the patient-facing routes, which we haven’t

01:31:43.619 --> 01:31:47.400
really had before.
The reason why that matters is because I think

01:31:47.400 --> 01:31:52.579
we should be quite encouraged about what we’re
going to then propose in the long-term plan

01:31:52.579 --> 01:31:58.219
in terms of further enhancements and development
of the digital offer, which will make a real

01:31:58.219 --> 01:32:03.830
choice available to different cohorts of patients
over the next three to four years.

01:32:03.830 --> 01:32:09.760
&gt;&gt;David Prior: I think – that’s a comment
rather than a question, I think, isn’t it?

01:32:09.760 --> 01:32:15.920
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: It was an echo of a theme,
which I wholly agree with.

01:32:15.920 --> 01:32:19.800
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah.
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Just a question, really.

01:32:19.800 --> 01:32:27.160
I’m sure that we’re doing this, but the
notion of tracking who, what, where, in relation

01:32:27.160 --> 01:32:34.670
to the digital app, it seems to me to be quite
an important piece of work. Are we doing that,

01:32:34.670 --> 01:32:40.170
or have we started doing it, so that we are
currently evaluating as we go in terms of,

01:32:40.170 --> 01:32:45.449
you know, inequalities, impact, that kind
of – who uses this stuff, what it really

01:32:45.449 --> 01:32:47.500
costs?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yeah, so on the digital

01:32:47.500 --> 01:32:53.909
element, we do a lot of work on who’s using
the systems, where are they using them, how

01:32:53.909 --> 01:32:58.880
long do they use them for, do – are they
having a look and walking away, or are they

01:32:58.880 --> 01:33:03.039
going through them? So, we’re gathering
– and a lot of that data, it’s not something

01:33:03.039 --> 01:33:09.070
– I think it’s a – it’s a world that
the NHS has not been in very well in the past,

01:33:09.070 --> 01:33:15.659
and I think on the digital solutions, we’re
much further advanced than in some other areas.

01:33:15.659 --> 01:33:24.280
And there are a raft of specific digital access
programmes that are being run in hard-to-reach

01:33:24.280 --> 01:33:28.969
communities as part of this, as well.
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: I understand that, yeah.

01:33:28.969 --> 01:33:35.099
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Both some very economically
poor communities, and ethnically diverse communities,

01:33:35.099 --> 01:33:40.690
and – so there are specific programmes running
around the country to try and make sure we

01:33:40.690 --> 01:33:46.670
address issues of digital divide and learn
from those in terms of how to build solutions

01:33:46.670 --> 01:33:50.989
that are appropriate for the technology that
people have access to and the lives that they

01:33:50.989 --> 01:33:52.590
live.
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Because in a way, that’d

01:33:52.590 --> 01:33:56.289
be the thing that – it’s not for me to
say, but it’d be interesting for the Board

01:33:56.289 --> 01:34:02.449
to see the systemic impacts of these interventions,
so that it can think about what this performance

01:34:02.449 --> 01:34:05.730
strategy looks like. I’d be fascinated to
see –

01:34:05.730 --> 01:34:10.760
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: I mean, I spent yesterday
afternoon with the President of the Royal

01:34:10.760 --> 01:34:14.860
College of Psychiatry talking about how digital
technology can be used to support patients

01:34:14.860 --> 01:34:19.860
with mental health problems.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you. Then we move on

01:34:19.860 --> 01:34:23.630
to winter.
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yes, thank you. So, as

01:34:23.630 --> 01:34:29.369
you’ll be aware, we published – well,
first of all, apologies from Pauline and Neil,

01:34:29.369 --> 01:34:35.880
who’ve both got better offers than this,
but I’m delighted to step in for them. The

01:34:35.880 --> 01:34:42.210
– you’ll be aware that the – our winter
review of last year was published back in

01:34:42.210 --> 01:34:47.210
September. Its conclusion was really that
the careful preparation and tremendous efforts

01:34:47.210 --> 01:34:53.739
from frontline staff meant that, despite the
fact that we were hit by the worst flu in

01:34:53.739 --> 01:35:01.320
seven years, more patients, people, were seen,
and either admitted or treated and discharged

01:35:01.320 --> 01:35:09.800
within four hours than ever before. And I
think – my personal view is that the upshot

01:35:09.800 --> 01:35:15.140
of all of that preparation and effort meant
that, whilst it got bumpy through winter,

01:35:15.140 --> 01:35:19.949
the NHS stayed on its feet, continued to deliver
high-quality care and handled winter. If we

01:35:19.949 --> 01:35:23.530
hadn’t been that well prepared, flu could
have put us in a very difficult – in a very

01:35:23.530 --> 01:35:28.280
different place. And if we hadn’t had the
flu, what we would have seen was a significant

01:35:28.280 --> 01:35:33.150
improvement in A&amp;E performance last winter.
So – and – so I think the winter review

01:35:33.150 --> 01:35:39.199
gave that kind of view.
Coming into this year, there’s been a continued

01:35:39.199 --> 01:35:46.579
focus on – throughout the summer and running
into the autumn, on working with the most

01:35:46.579 --> 01:35:53.469
underperforming trusts to try and improve
their performance, making sure that we have

01:35:53.469 --> 01:35:59.719
GP out of hours and winter cover properly
in place, making sure that GP streaming is

01:35:59.719 --> 01:36:06.789
in place in every A&amp;E, making sure that 111
is able to handle the new volumes and provide

01:36:06.789 --> 01:36:12.429
the right levels of clinical contact, and
really continuing to roll out the urgent and

01:36:12.429 --> 01:36:15.909
emergency care strategy and the integrated
urgent care strategies that we’ve had for

01:36:15.909 --> 01:36:19.389
the last couple of years, and I think there
have been – there’s been a real advantage

01:36:19.389 --> 01:36:24.250
in having a strategy which we have enhanced
each year, as opposed to publishing a whole

01:36:24.250 --> 01:36:29.000
new strategy every year. There is a consistency
to the model.

01:36:29.000 --> 01:36:35.199
As we look at winter going forwards, coming
rapidly towards us now – you look out of

01:36:35.199 --> 01:36:42.780
the window, it’s kind of here – we have
a more effective flu vaccine than we had last

01:36:42.780 --> 01:36:50.860
year. We saw an increase of 5% in the number
of NHS staff who were vaccinated last year.

01:36:50.860 --> 01:36:57.309
We have a big focus on getting NHS staff vaccinated
this year, a whole new set of requirements.

01:36:57.309 --> 01:37:03.289
I think we have raised the level of expectation
quite significantly. We have some organisations

01:37:03.289 --> 01:37:08.659
that are doing spectacularly well, and get
the vast majority of their staff vaccinated.

01:37:08.659 --> 01:37:15.400
And then other organisations who do pitifully,
and we do need to see every organisation step

01:37:15.400 --> 01:37:18.559
up and do the right thing for their staff
and for their patients. And I –

01:37:18.559 --> 01:37:20.449
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: Yeah, absolutely.
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: And so, I think that

01:37:20.449 --> 01:37:26.539
is critical to us.
But in terms of the public, we have – all

01:37:26.539 --> 01:37:29.840
of the vaccine that we’re expecting to be
delivered has been delivered. It’s been

01:37:29.840 --> 01:37:38.360
delivered as was planned and as primary care
and pharmacists knew. The noise about vaccine

01:37:38.360 --> 01:37:43.940
shortages we dealt with by making sure that
we knew exactly where all the vaccines were,

01:37:43.940 --> 01:37:48.449
all over the country. In some places, we moved
vaccines around, but we now have all of the

01:37:48.449 --> 01:37:55.719
vaccine out there. In fact, we have a&nbsp;million
more doses of vaccine now in the NHS than

01:37:55.719 --> 01:38:01.960
we gave to people last year, so there is no
reason why we shouldn’t see – why anybody

01:38:01.960 --> 01:38:06.769
shouldn’t be able to get access to the vaccine,
and we would urge people now, if you haven’t

01:38:06.769 --> 01:38:09.920
been – had your vaccine and you’re part
of one of our risk and vulnerable groups,

01:38:09.920 --> 01:38:19.210
now is the moment to go forwards.
We’ve had a – the focus for this year’s

01:38:19.210 --> 01:38:24.480
winter plan has been – probably the single
biggest part of it has been on beds. It’s

01:38:24.480 --> 01:38:31.789
been on, is there the capacity to create enough
– a low enough bed occupancy level that

01:38:31.789 --> 01:38:41.050
we can create flow through hospitals? We set
out to try and release 4,000 beds through

01:38:41.050 --> 01:38:48.449
a reduction in long-stay patients. We’ve
released about 2,500 so far, so the focus

01:38:48.449 --> 01:38:54.590
has to continue on that in order to continue
to say, patients who are inappropriately in

01:38:54.590 --> 01:38:58.880
hospital and would be better cared for somewhere
else, let’s get the packages of care – whether

01:38:58.880 --> 01:39:01.369
they’re provided by social care, whether
they’re provided by the NHS – in place,

01:39:01.369 --> 01:39:05.040
so we can get them to a better location in
order to create the space in our hospitals

01:39:05.040 --> 01:39:13.230
so that people who need to be admitted can
be admitted. And we need also to ensure that

01:39:13.230 --> 01:39:18.260
beds that have been closed in hospitals over
the summer are now opened going into winter.

01:39:18.260 --> 01:39:24.099
We need – we have seen a bit of an inclination
of, as length of stay has come down, as beds

01:39:24.099 --> 01:39:27.190
– as hospitals have become more efficient,
some of those beds have been being closed

01:39:27.190 --> 01:39:33.329
behind them. We now need to get those beds
opened. The NHS needs that capacity.

01:39:33.329 --> 01:39:42.130
We’ve invested £145&nbsp;million in A&amp;Es, in
creating extra bed capacity and creating facilities

01:39:42.130 --> 01:39:49.219
for same-day A&amp;E treatment, or A&amp;E day cases.
One of the big expansions that we’ve seen

01:39:49.219 --> 01:39:54.869
this year is in particular pathways where
patients can be managed in A&amp;E and discharged

01:39:54.869 --> 01:40:01.630
the same day, rather than being admitted to
a ward, frequently an elderly care ward, where

01:40:01.630 --> 01:40:05.469
it might then take you two or three or four
days to get them back discharged, and I can

01:40:05.469 --> 01:40:09.360
say from my personal family experience, is
that when that is done well, it works really,

01:40:09.360 --> 01:40:12.760
really well. So, we’re seeing that across
the country.

01:40:12.760 --> 01:40:18.510
We’re also working very closely with our
colleagues in social care to ensure that the

01:40:18.510 --> 01:40:25.179
£240&nbsp;million that has been put into social
care to fund staffing and packages of care,

01:40:25.179 --> 01:40:30.179
in order to ensure that people aren’t inappropriately
in hospital, is spent well and effectively,

01:40:30.179 --> 01:40:36.679
and is actually creating that flow-through
through hospital. We’re confident that we

01:40:36.679 --> 01:40:42.900
will have the extended access to primary care
in place. We are confident that we are building

01:40:42.900 --> 01:40:48.540
the capacity in 111 to continue to manage
the sort of growth that we’ve seen. But

01:40:48.540 --> 01:40:56.170
there’s a very close level of scrutiny now.
We have somebody overseeing the winter plan

01:40:56.170 --> 01:41:01.940
delivery in each of the regions, as their
full-time job. And Pauline and her team are

01:41:01.940 --> 01:41:08.360
doing a series of calls over the next two
weeks, so 92 trusts and their associated CCG

01:41:08.360 --> 01:41:11.789
accountable officers – so this is chief
executives and accountable officers – will

01:41:11.789 --> 01:41:15.929
be walking through with Pauline and her team
exactly what they now have in place for winter,

01:41:15.929 --> 01:41:19.619
what needs to be done over the next couple
of weeks, and how we’re going to make sure

01:41:19.619 --> 01:41:26.070
that we run through from this point now through
to sort of mid-February, how is this going

01:41:26.070 --> 01:41:33.390
to be delivered, and out of that, identify
those trusts or those CCGs that need more

01:41:33.390 --> 01:41:39.800
help from NHS England and NHSI in order to
make sure we get the delivery right. But the

01:41:39.800 --> 01:41:46.610
– I think it would be fair to say that Pauline
Phillips is person-marking every single A&amp;E

01:41:46.610 --> 01:41:51.420
department across the country now.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Right. Thanks very much. Richard?

01:41:51.420 --> 01:41:56.269
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: Just a quick question on
the £240&nbsp;million social care. Because we

01:41:56.269 --> 01:42:01.760
have had a problem in the past with making
best use of monies that come out relatively

01:42:01.760 --> 01:42:03.925
late in the day. Can you say a little bit
about how that money’s being used, and how

01:42:03.925 --> 01:42:08.099
convinced we are about the effectiveness of
that?

01:42:08.099 --> 01:42:14.780
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Well, I think first thing
to say is that relationships between health

01:42:14.780 --> 01:42:21.170
and social care are spectacularly better now
than they probably were a couple of years

01:42:21.170 --> 01:42:25.500
ago, that the work over the last couple of
years does mean that there – and as I travel

01:42:25.500 --> 01:42:31.440
around the country and talk to STPs, there’s
almost always – so, I was in – I was up

01:42:31.440 --> 01:42:36.469
in Huddersfield last Friday, and the Calderdale
and Huddersfield directors of adult social

01:42:36.469 --> 01:42:39.580
care were in the room with the chief executive
of the local hospital and the accountable

01:42:39.580 --> 01:42:44.329
officers for the CCG. So, that is now much
more than norm. So, I think there is much

01:42:44.329 --> 01:42:49.550
more a sense of partnership, of us being in
this together. Notwithstanding that we are

01:42:49.550 --> 01:42:56.730
expecting regions and CCGs to ensure that
this money does turn into actual packages

01:42:56.730 --> 01:43:06.040
of care for real people, in order to deliver
the flow-through. And we will know whether

01:43:06.040 --> 01:43:12.861
it works or not, but that’s a – I think
that’s a – it’s a local management piece,

01:43:12.861 --> 01:43:18.270
and part of what Pauline and the emergency
care team are doing is saying, ‘So, what

01:43:18.270 --> 01:43:22.659
is that doing? Is it actually moving real
patients from inappropriate hospital beds

01:43:22.659 --> 01:43:26.179
into appropriate packages of care in their
own home?’

01:43:26.179 --> 01:43:34.650
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Any other questions for Matthew?
Well, there’s a lot going on, Matthew. Well,

01:43:34.650 --> 01:43:37.159
thank you very much, anyway, for that report.
Many thanks.

01:43:37.159 --> 01:43:42.110
So, Matt, finance?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Style: Thank you. So, the – Chairman,

01:43:42.110 --> 01:43:51.350
the paper before the Board reflects the year-to-date
forecast position at month six 2018/19. As

01:43:51.350 --> 01:43:58.000
reported at the September Board meeting, the
commissioning sector has revised its plan

01:43:58.000 --> 01:44:06.199
and is planning to deliver an underspend of
£265&nbsp;million for ‘18/19, and year-to-date

01:44:06.199 --> 01:44:12.750
performance and full-year forecast at the
end of month six is broadly in line with that.

01:44:12.750 --> 01:44:20.630
Within that, sort of under the bonnet, as
it were, there were 36 CCGs with a year-to-date

01:44:20.630 --> 01:44:29.010
overspend. Those are largely driven by overspends
on acute contracts, and those are offset by

01:44:29.010 --> 01:44:33.521
underspends within our direct commissioning
budgets and within NHS England’s central

01:44:33.521 --> 01:44:40.349
budgets. And we’re expecting – at month
six, we’re expecting all bar 11 of those

01:44:40.349 --> 01:44:48.289
36 CCGs to bring their full-year position
in line with plan by the end of the year.

01:44:48.289 --> 01:44:56.230
The – I mentioned the central budgets were
offsetting some overspends in CCGs. That’s

01:44:56.230 --> 01:45:03.719
largely driven by a combination of vacancies,
some unexpected income from GP rates rebates,

01:45:03.719 --> 01:45:11.500
counterfraud receipts, and similar small items.
I will – as I said, the paper reflects the

01:45:11.500 --> 01:45:18.869
position at month six. Since the papers were
prepared, we have, as we always do at this

01:45:18.869 --> 01:45:24.019
point of the year, conducted a sort of thorough
but routine review of our central budgets

01:45:24.019 --> 01:45:31.420
in order to identify any possible further
underspends, and to give you an update on

01:45:31.420 --> 01:45:38.940
that in this meeting. Taking those further
likely underspends in central budgets, combining

01:45:38.940 --> 01:45:44.340
those with our overall assessment of risk
out in the system and our ability to manage

01:45:44.340 --> 01:45:48.789
that, and indeed, taking account of some of
the items that have been discussed earlier

01:45:48.789 --> 01:45:54.139
on in the meeting, both around the timely
conclusion of the PPRS deal, and the successful

01:45:54.139 --> 01:46:02.560
procurements around biosimilar medicines,
we think that for month seven, we’ll be

01:46:02.560 --> 01:46:09.179
able to release a number of further reserves
and contingencies into the reported position.

01:46:09.179 --> 01:46:14.659
And taking those together with the position
on direct commissioning and updated forecasts

01:46:14.659 --> 01:46:22.969
of performance against the quality premium,
we would expect to be, in our next report,

01:46:22.969 --> 01:46:26.750
forecasting a year-end underspend of at least
£450&nbsp;million.

01:46:26.750 --> 01:46:33.769
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Matthew. Normally,
when you have your – the new finance director

01:46:33.769 --> 01:46:38.659
comes up with bad news, but it seems to be
going the right way this time. So, thank you

01:46:38.659 --> 01:46:40.889
for that. Any questions? David, have you got
any questions?

01:46:40.889 --> 01:46:46.850
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Just one, Matt. The – your
confidence in the CCG forecasts. You’ve

01:46:46.850 --> 01:46:54.400
done your usual sort of scrubbing of the numbers
and so on, but is there much downside risk

01:46:54.400 --> 01:46:59.280
within there, or are you comfortable?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Style: I mean, I think our regions

01:46:59.280 --> 01:47:04.969
in particular have done a very thorough deep
dive over the month-five, month-six period,

01:47:04.969 --> 01:47:11.940
in order to increase our confidence in those
forecasts. Clearly, there is risk in the position,

01:47:11.940 --> 01:47:20.230
and as I say, particularly reflected in the
acute contracting position and volume risk

01:47:20.230 --> 01:47:26.230
there. At the same time, we have worked hard
to identify mitigation, so that I’m confident

01:47:26.230 --> 01:47:31.179
we’ll be able to deliver the position as
reflected in the month-six forecast.

01:47:31.179 --> 01:47:38.590
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much, Matt. So,
on the next item, which is governance, I think

01:47:38.590 --> 01:47:41.909
we’ve got reports from the various Board
committees. I don’t think there’s any

01:47:41.909 --> 01:47:48.300
issues arising from them, is there? Has anyone
got any issue they wanted to raise on that?

01:47:48.300 --> 01:47:51.260
Thank you.
I think we’re onto any other business. I’d

01:47:51.260 --> 01:47:56.420
just like to say a few words about Victor,
because Victor can’t come to the Board event

01:47:56.420 --> 01:48:02.230
this evening. So, no-one will be saying anything
about him then, so I’d –

01:48:02.230 --> 01:48:08.510
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Well, I don’t know…
Please, people, feel free.

01:48:08.510 --> 01:48:13.920
&gt;&gt;David Prior: So, I’ve known Victor for
I think about four years, and it was a – actually,

01:48:13.920 --> 01:48:18.039
it was a meeting in the House of Lords when
I was at the CQC, where I first met Victor.

01:48:18.039 --> 01:48:23.889
And I think if there were two things where
Victor – and there are many more things,

01:48:23.889 --> 01:48:31.190
but two things where I think Victor has made
a real difference to the NHS over the – and

01:48:31.190 --> 01:48:36.699
this is looking at it from the outside, in
my case, the first would be around the workforce

01:48:36.699 --> 01:48:44.059
race equality standards, and raising the whole,
I think, put it – well, the issue of the

01:48:44.059 --> 01:48:48.280
sort of – the lack of inclusion of people
from BME backgrounds in senior positions in

01:48:48.280 --> 01:48:55.559
the NHS, which is a huge issue. And I think
really, it’s almost a metaphor for the lack

01:48:55.559 --> 01:48:59.520
of inclusion throughout the NHS, that we can
do so much better in this area. And if we

01:48:59.520 --> 01:49:05.370
can crack the BME issue, then we’ll crack
every other issue in our – in the sort of

01:49:05.370 --> 01:49:12.739
workforce culture within the NHS. And so you’ve
done a huge amount in that area, and I hope

01:49:12.739 --> 01:49:16.619
you’ll carry on doing that, working with
Yvonne and others.

01:49:16.619 --> 01:49:22.579
The second is – well, actually – and I
would say that in terms of the argument for

01:49:22.579 --> 01:49:27.780
diversity, I mean, the role that you’ve
played on this Board is sort of living proof

01:49:27.780 --> 01:49:34.730
of that, that you’ll never get groupthink
if Victor is on the – is in the group. It

01:49:34.730 --> 01:49:45.059
is that mixture of sort of iconoclastic, irreverent
behaviour, coupled with that sort of moral

01:49:45.059 --> 01:49:49.349
authority that you bring to it from your own
background at Turning Point and all the work

01:49:49.349 --> 01:49:54.289
you’ve done before. And so I think the second
area where you’ve made a huge impact is

01:49:54.289 --> 01:50:01.610
on never letting go of the issues around inequalities,
making the case that – actually, the moral

01:50:01.610 --> 01:50:08.440
case for the NHS. If it’s not about solving
inequalities or reducing inequalities, then

01:50:08.440 --> 01:50:12.889
there is no moral case for the NHS, and I
think putting that on the table all the time,

01:50:12.889 --> 01:50:17.329
and I think we’ll see it reflected very
strongly in the ten-year plan. It’s another

01:50:17.329 --> 01:50:25.570
huge tribute and reflection of the value you’ve
brought to this Board and to the NHS more

01:50:25.570 --> 01:50:29.079
widely.
Just on a slightly humorous note, just ending,

01:50:29.079 --> 01:50:34.770
I remember when Victor told me when he went
to the committee about whether or not he should

01:50:34.770 --> 01:50:39.230
go into the House of Lords, he was asked,
‘Why do you want to be in the House of Lords?’

01:50:39.230 --> 01:50:44.999
And he replied, well, ‘Free car parking
in Central London’, which tells you something

01:50:44.999 --> 01:50:51.450
about the irreverence of – but also, you
know, because it’s not true, because that

01:50:51.450 --> 01:50:56.659
is not true in Victor’s case, because actually,
what he wanted to do was bring the voice of

01:50:56.659 --> 01:51:00.869
people from his background right to the centre
of sort of government thinking and government

01:51:00.869 --> 01:51:06.289
policy, and you’ve done that in spades,
Victor, if I may say so. So, on behalf of

01:51:06.289 --> 01:51:11.080
all of us in this Board, a huge thank you
for all you’ve done, and I’m sure you’ll

01:51:11.080 --> 01:51:16.159
carry on making a contribution in a different
guise in the future, Victor. So, thank you

01:51:16.159 --> 01:51:18.440
very much.
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Thank you very much.

01:51:18.440 --> 01:51:23.940
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you. So, I think we now
have a resolution to move to a private part

01:51:23.940 --> 01:51:28.330
of the Board, which we’ll do. So, thank
you everyone who’s come today. Thank you.

